{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/jolt/1794006000000-architect-j6g-ruling.gpt-6.1-sol.ruling.md collab/jolt/1794087000000-engineer-j6g-index-incremental.findings.md BRIEF-j6g-fix.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: filed by the orchestrator at the round's close\nCoding-Agent: codex gpt-6.1-sol (architect-review, read-only; session eb3d92db)\nSession-ID: eb3d92db-346b-43f6-a29d-77603374c3ca\n\n**J6g ruling — Session-ID: eb3d92db-346b-43f6-a29d-77603374c3ca**\n\n**Disposition:** J6g remains **FAILED**: 79 samples is below 600, and p99 ≈7.9 s exceeds 250 ms. Delivered-once and REPL advancement satisfy only their respective legs. The criterion stands. No code changes or remote runs were performed in this ruling round.\n\n**1. Scheduling principle**\n\n**A1’s transfer-count bounds do not bound an owner round’s synchronous interpretation or the elapsed interval between peer visits.** One read, one write, and correct descriptor rotation constrain operations per visit. They do not constrain compilation, expansion, indexing, publication, VM execution, message assembly, or callbacks executed between visits or within one visit.\n\nThe owner-thread contract shall state:\n\n> A composition claiming bounded service intervals must bound the synchronous work along every path between service opportunities. Work that can exceed that budget must return an explicit successor for later owner advancement. Transfer-count or instruction-count bounds alone establish no wall-time guarantee.\n\nChecking a deadline after an unbounded call returns does not enforce that deadline. Likewise, VM fuel cannot bound an unbounded host operation inside one instruction.\n\nThe source establishes a concrete violation of any history-independent interpretation bound: [yin.repl.index](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-orch/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:21) explicitly documents full-history publication and repeated watermark scanning, with quadratic total session cost. A finite five-iteration guest loop does not bound that surrounding work.\n\nDescriptor rotation is not ruled defective. The filed diagnosis establishes the growing source-level cost; it explicitly does **not** identify the historical hot instruction from remote stack evidence. The trace must supply that attribution.\n\n**2. Selected architecture and fix order**\n\n**Select (a), incremental index materialization, as the primary remedy for the confirmed history-growth mechanism. Select bounded advancement as the scheduling requirement wherever residual synchronous work exceeds the service budget. Do not select (c) without a measured assembly hotspot.**\n\nThe implementation order is evidence-driven:\n\n1. **Run the attribution trace before performance edits.** If code indexing dominates, proceed with (a). If another phase dominates, investigate that phase before treating index optimization as the sufficient J6g remedy.\n2. **Implement (a) when indexing attribution confirms it.** Then trace again to identify residual monopolization.\n3. **Apply (b) to the residual execution or publication work that requires yielding.** A VM-only change cannot repair synchronous publication before the VM runs.\n4. **Consider (c) only when pump timing and stack evidence identify payload processing near A’s final bytes.** That evidence may justify prioritizing this branch; it is not a mandatory third optimization.\n\nFor **(a)**, carry the publication cursor, covered roots, and allocator successor as explicit composition-owned derived state. Consume the committed suffix rather than reopening and reconstructing the entire history each evaluation. Retaining an allocator successor must preserve transactor authority and derive/validate it from authoritative history on recovery; the shell must not invent transaction times.\n\nThis ruling supersedes acceptance of repeated full-history rebuilding as the necessary price of every-evaluation publication. It **preserves that publication cadence**. Every successful publication must cover the committed frontier, materialize its required rows and index content, validate the manifest, and complete the applicable HEAD procedure before reporting publication success. Preserve atomic transactions, provenance, recovery, retry, and failure reporting. No history truncation, hidden global cache, canonical tuple field, or periodic-publication substitution is selected.\n\nIncrementality removes repeated reconstruction; it does **not** by itself prove a fixed service interval. Tree operations, validation, encoding, hashing, and storage work still require measurement and, where necessary, bounded advancement.\n\nFor **(b)**, retain pending execution or publication as an explicit successor advanced by the same owner. No worker thread, callback scheduler, or nested socket polling is selected.\n\nQuery visibility is ruled as follows:\n\n- While committed code remains unpublished, ordinary session queries return an explicit index-unavailable/pending refusal. They must not silently substitute the previous manifest.\n- Do not expose partially built indexes or combine published datoms with partially advanced AST relations.\n- Preserve publication-before-evaluation ordering for successful indexing. Publication failure continues through the existing explicit failure policy; yielding must not turn pending work into success.\n- Keep the previous verified manifest available for recovery and explicitly addressed snapshot use, without presenting it as the current session frontier.\n\nThis follows the existing [query snapshot contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-orch/src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:405).\n\nA yielded VM quantum is not evidence of a completed W evaluation. Preserve the registered submission and progress checks. If resumability requires changing what “every owner round” means in the harness, that requires a separate explicit workload ruling; it cannot silently certify this gate.\n\nFor **(c)**, a slow `:server-pump` phase alone is insufficient: it includes waiting, parsing, assembly, and callbacks. Require stack evidence in `take-payload`, `finish-frame`, `concat-chunks`, or their byte operations. A subsequent scoped change must preserve owned payloads, pure parser successors, message/control bounds, ordering, close behavior, and exactly-once complete-message delivery. Resumable assembly must withhold delivery until complete and expose runnable work through the existing deadline seam.\n\n**No adapter/parser change is selected on the present evidence. A1–A7 remain binding.**\n\n**3. Traced-run protocol and acceptance**\n\nOn the qualified remote checkout, record the commit, working diff, host, Jolt/native qualification, command, and configuration. Preserve stdout, stderr, and exit status.\n\nFirst verify instrumentation presence. This local checkout has the older first-round `W-PHASE` markers, but lacks the filed `JOLT_WORKLOAD_W_TRACE` implementation and nested phase/age reporting. An environment variable alone cannot establish that the remote checkout contains it.\n\nRun:\n\n```sh\nJOLT_WORKLOAD_W_TRACE=1 bb workload-w:jolt workload-w\n```\n\nThe trace must provide:\n\n- Flushed enter/leave records with round and monotonic timestamps for REPL work, A send, B send, server wait/pump, and client wait/pump; nested code-index and AST-index records.\n- Phase counts, total/max durations, retained transaction growth, owner rounds, verified REPL results, B sent/completed/outstanding counts, and A sent/delivered counts.\n- A’s first positive write and first delivery timestamps, with B samples restricted to requests sent and answered inside that interval.\n- Independent supervisor reporting of active nested phase, round, and age every ten seconds and at the unchanged 300,000 ms whole-run cap.\n\nParent timings include child timings; do not add them as independent costs. Obtain CPU stack samples during sustained slow phases to distinguish scanning/building from hashing, GC, VM work, assembly, callbacks, or native polling. Missing B submissions indicate owner/producer cadence failure, not automatically lost network replies.\n\n**The traced run is diagnostic and cannot PASS J6g.** Its intentional nonzero status must be distinguished from watchdog or worker failure.\n\nAfter the selected fix lands, repeat attribution and then run the unchanged, untraced gate:\n\n```sh\nbb workload-w:jolt workload-w\n```\n\nJ6g passes only when all registered conditions hold together:\n\n- A’s 4 MiB message is delivered exactly once.\n- At least **600** qualifying overlapping B round trips complete, with **p99 ≤250 ms** and no lost reply.\n- The required REPL evaluation advances every owner round, verified through actual shell/result progress.\n- The registered transfer pacing, duration, offered load, and outstanding-request limit are preserved.\n\nPost-transfer replies cannot fill the sample target. The 500-sample validity floor is not a pass threshold. A watchdog timeout, incomplete evidence, or any unmet leg leaves J6g unpassed. The five-minute cap is a safety limit, not a replacement transfer window.Completed-GMT: 2026-11-04 06:24 UTC\nCoding-Agent: codex gpt-6.1-sol (implementation, edits only)\nSession-ID: 0d3f8e24-7a51-4c93-8b26-1f9e6a3d5c80\n\n# J6g incremental code-index materialization\n\nImplemented the ruled composition change. This is an **ungated edits-only\ndelivery**: no runtime test, build, remote command, workload, commit, staging,\nor push was performed. The orchestrator owns remote verification and kimi's\ngate. No J6g performance pass is claimed.\n\n## Checkout and scope\n\nThe supplied directory is on `jolt-orch2`, not `jolt-j6g`. The requested\n`git pull --rebase` completed and answered `Already up to date`; the base is\n`ff369c57db46efffee47f119fe68555b34b1f727`, which contains `1cddaa93`.\nThe first sandboxed pull could not write linked-worktree Git metadata; the\nsame authorized operation succeeded with sandbox escalation. Existing\nuntracked files were left alone.\n\nChanged the three allowed production files, their index/transactor tests,\nand the explicitly allowed W cursor observation. The harness's only semantic\nchange prints `index-cursor` in its existing telemetry. cljstyle also corrected\npre-existing whitespace in that file. The watchdog, pacing, submissions,\nsample rules, transfer size, thresholds, and stop conditions are unchanged.\n`src/jolt/**`, the parser, `dao.space.index`, and query code are unchanged.\n\nThe harness on this base still has the older first-round `W-PHASE` reporting;\nit does not contain the diagnostic `W-PROGRESS`/nested trace implementation\ndescribed in the ruling. This round does not port or alter that instrumentation.\nRemote attribution must use the orchestrator's qualified instrumented harness.\n\n## State and advancement\n\n`yin.repl.index/make-indexer` now has two explicit derived fields:\n\n```clojure\n{:allocator <live transactor authority, :intake-pool nil>\n :materialization {:cursor <verified local-log successor>\n                   :transactions <covered transaction-record count>\n                   :manifest-address <verified manifest address>\n                   :manifest <manifest with all four covered root addresses>\n                   :max-t <greatest committed transaction time>}}\n```\n\nThe allocator is initially created by `transactor/create!`, using its existing\nauthoritative watermark scan. `transactor/with-intake-pool` reuses the same\nallocation lock, next-t and closed-state cells while replacing or detaching\nthe publication intake. It is the same writer authority; the shell supplies\nno transaction time. `transactor/watermark` exposes that authority's next\ntime as a read-only diagnostic, never a recovery input. An allocator bound\nto another local handle is discarded in favor of `create!`.\n\nInitial publication uses today's `transactor/publish!` full reconstruction.\nLater publications lazily restore the previously verified roots with the\nexisting `dao.space.index/restore`, read from the retained opaque cursor,\nand feed only the appended transaction records into `index/fold-batch` in\n`:resolved` mode. `index/publish!` emits the dirty subgraph, children first,\nthen its manifest through the round's fresh complete-retention intake.\nNo full prefix scan, full sorting, or whole-tree rebuild occurs on the\nnormal incremental path. Temporary trees, recorder, intake, pool and staged\npayloads are dropped at the round boundary; only the fields above survive.\n\nPublication remains synchronous on every evaluation that commits code, and\n`commit-names` uses the same path. Program rows still materialize before\npublication. Each emitted node is now also hash-verified/read back through\n`jing/get` as the intake drains; the manifest gets the existing validated\n`read-manifest` check. Previously verified immutable retained subgraphs do\nnot need another whole-graph traversal. Only after all of this and the\napplicable HEAD write succeed does the new cursor/manifest become published.\nFailed publication leaves the previous frontier intact. A later committing\nround replays the entire unpublished suffix from that frontier, using a\nfresh temporary builder; successful retry clears a publication failure.\nQueries keep their existing explicit refusal while the frontier is behind\nor a failure/loss exists; no reader substitutes the old frontier as current.\n\n`repl-state` exposes `:index-publication` containing only the cursor, covered\ntransaction count and manifest address. Existing `index/status` shape is\npreserved. W observes the cursor directly from its owned shell state.\n\n## Recovery and cursor validation\n\nNo process cursor or allocator is persisted or accepted on restart.\n`rehydrate` reconstructs a fresh complete-retention log from authoritative\nrecovered datoms grouped by original transaction time, preserves those\ntimes/provenance, resumes entity allocation above every recovered e/m, and\nexplicitly clears both derived fields. This also discards a stale field on\nan indexer passed to `rehydrate`, not just fields supplied in recovery data.\nThe previous verified manifest continues to serve recovery queries as today.\nThe first subsequent committing round derives its allocator from this log\nand uses full reconstruction before installing a newly derived tail cursor.\n\nFor a live frontier, manifest address, covered record count and branching\nfactor must match. The suffix must finish at blocked/end after **exactly**\n`transactions - covered-transactions` valid, admitted transaction records.\nAn earlier cursor yields too many records; an ahead cursor yields too few;\na foreign/invalid cursor is rejected by the transport. None of those cases\ncan publish the uncertain suffix: all take today's full reconstruction path.\nMalformed authoritative history still fails that path's validation. Cursor\nvalidation neither inspects nor fabricates transport-specific cursor slots.\nIn-process durable reset/VM selection carry the live successors with the\nsame retained log; a worker restart always takes the derivation path above.\n\n## Equivalence tests and review adjustment\n\nAdded portable tests (written, **not executed** in this edits-only round):\n\n- Six rounds, three packets per round, repeated content and branching factor\n  four: compare the complete ordered facts under **each** covered root and\n  the manifest count with today's independent full-history publisher.\n- Twelve rounds with branching factor sixteen: exact manifest/four-root\n  **address equality** against a full insertion replay, including splits.\n- A fresh evaluation after three publications must make no `:oldest`\n  cursor request, read exactly one suffix record plus blocked, and preserve\n  the transactor's allocation authority. An explicit reader/writer wrapper\n  counts these operations; no host-specific `with-redefs` spy is used.\n- Stale, ahead and foreign live cursors must trigger full reconstruction;\n  its resulting manifest must exactly equal today's full publisher.\n- Recovery discards stale live successors, reconstructs from history, and\n  the first recovered publication exactly matches today's full publisher.\n- Recovery of times 7 and 43 must allocate 44 next, rather than using the\n  record count, and must advance entity allocation above restored e/m.\n- Unreadable newly emitted content and failed HEAD writes must leave the\n  previous cursor and HEAD intact; retry covers all unpublished transactions.\n- Retargeted/detached transactors preserve atomic allocation, refusal/retry\n  watermarks and close behavior. Existing retention assertions now require\n  derived successors while still excluding prior intake/pool payloads.\n\n**Review adjustment:** “identical covered roots” needs a distinction between\nidentical covered relations and identical Merkle addresses. Today's full\npublisher uses `bt/from-sequential`, which sorts/deduplicates and bulk-packs\nnodes (`from-sorted-array`/`split-ranges`); incremental `bt/conj` splits nodes\nas insertions arrive. These valid trees can have different shapes and root\naddresses for the same datoms. This delivery tests relation equivalence\nagainst the unchanged bulk publisher, and exact address equivalence against\nfull insertion replay. It does **not** claim general address equality with\nbulk packing. This interpretation was raised with the user during the round;\nno reply had arrived when filing. If the brief requires byte-for-byte equality\nto bulk-packed roots, that requirement remains unresolved and needs an\narchitecture decision; the reference publisher was not silently changed.\n\nRecovery deliberately reconstructs fully once instead of introducing a new\ndurable checkpoint/sidecar format. Existing immutable manifests remain the\ndurable authority. No canonical tuple or manifest field was added.\n\n## Checks and remaining gates\n\n- `cljstyle fix`, then `cljstyle check`, all six changed Clojure files: exit 0.\n- `git diff --check`: exit 0.\n- No `collab/` references introduced into source/test comments.\n- No adapter/parser changes, test execution, runtime performance evidence,\n  publication yielding, owner scheduling change, or new query visibility rule.\n\nRemote verification should run the existing `yin.repl.index-test` and\n`dao.space.transactor-test` namespaces plus affected durable recovery/query\ntests (`yin.repl.store-test`, `yin.repl.query-test`), and the applicable\nportable lanes. Then repeat attributed W followed by the unchanged untraced\ngate on the qualified checkout, preserving the gate's overlap and progress\nrequirements. The equivalence assertions above are pending runtime evidence.\nIncrementality does not establish a 250 ms owner-service bound. Any residual\npublication monopolization requiring yielding belongs to a follow-up owner\nadvancement/query-visibility ruling, not this implementation.\n# J6g fix round: the workload W run hangs — attribute, guard, and re-measure\n\n(Re-pasted from the orchestrator's dispatch; the agy rounds died twice on\nthis unit, so this fix round is dispatched to codex per the terminal-state\nrule's escalation.)\n\n## What happened (the orchestrator's verification run, jupiter, j6ggate18)\n\nThe workload W harness compiled and ran: server listening, peer B\nconnected and upgraded, peer A connected and upgraded, frame length\n4,194,318 bytes, \"Entering owner round loop...\" — then 100% CPU spin for\n69 minutes, no output flush, no transfer completion. Killed at rc=143.\n\nThe pre-registered criterion (BRIEF-j6g.md, unchanged): A's 4 MiB message\ndelivered once within a ~60 s window while B samples p99 ≤ 250 ms with\n≥ 600 samples. Nothing measurable was produced.\n\n## Your tasks\n\n1. **Attribute the spin.** Two hypotheses — distinguish them with reads\n   (you cannot run anything):\n   (a) a harness defect: `write-all!`'s retry loop (`poll-one fd pollout\n   2000` then `send-chunk!`) spins if poll returns writable while send\n   answers :would-block, or if the loop never advances off 0; and there\n   is no max-runtime guard, so any stall hangs forever.\n   (b) a real composition finding: the REPL's owner round serves peer B\n   but never drains peer A's 4 MiB transfer (the exact starvation\n   workload W exists to expose).\n   Read src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj's write-all!/run-workload-w loop and\n   the driver's owner round in src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc (the\n   sleep-fds! + step path). State which you conclude, with file:line.\n2. **Fix the harness defects** regardless of (b):\n   - a max-runtime guard: the whole workload terminates with a FAILURE\n     report (p99 absent, samples counted, elapsed) after a hard cap\n     (5 minutes), never hanging;\n   - the write-all! loop gets a no-progress guard: N consecutive\n     :would-block polls without a byte advancing = transfer stalled →\n     report and abort the transfer leg (this is data: the criterion\n     failing, honestly measured);\n   - the sampler flushes its running p99/sample-count to stdout every\n     10 s so a stuck run leaves evidence.\n3. **If (b) is your conclusion** (the adapter starves A): do NOT patch\n   the adapter — report it as the finding with the reads that support it.\n   The Architect rules on adapter changes.\n4. cljstyle clean; no collab/ path in comments.\n\n## Reporting\n\nWrite BRIEF-j6g-fix-findings.md in this workspace with: the attribution,\nthe guards added, and what the next verification run should show under\neach hypothesis.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.repl.main\n  \"Host entry points for the DaoStream Yin REPL.\n\n   The one thing this namespace adds to `yin.repl.driver` is cadence, which\n   the DaoStream contract leaves to the runtime.  Per host, a line producer\n   appends what was typed to the input medium, nudges the step owner's wake\n   source, and returns; one ticker owns the state value and calls\n   `repl-step` serially at the interval `cadence-step` computes.  There is\n   no second state owner, and no line handler evaluates, requests, polls,\n   or prints.\"\n  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:async\" :as async]\n                       [\"dart:convert\" :as convert]\n                       [\"dart:core\" :as dart-core]\n                       [\"dart:io\" :as io]]\n                :jolt [[dao.host.text :as text]\n                       [dao.posix.file :as posix-file]\n                       [yang.python.antlr.parser :as python.antlr.parser]\n                       [yin.repl.host.common :as host-common]])\n            [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.space.store.fs :as fs]\n            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram]\n            [dao.stream.remote-channel :as remote-channel]\n            [dao.stream.waitset.cadence :as cadence]\n            [dao.stream.waitset.driver :as wake]\n            [yin.repl :as shell]\n            [yin.repl.connect :as connect]\n            [yin.repl.dht :as repl.dht]\n            [yin.repl.frontends :as repl.frontends]\n            [yin.repl.driver :as driver]\n            [yin.repl.host :as host]\n            [yin.repl.serve :as serve]\n            [yin.repl.state :as state]\n            [yin.repl.store :as store]\n            [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.compose :as compose]))\n\n\n(def tick-millis\n  \"The base cadence of the single step owner.  Slow enough to cost nothing\n   while anything moves, fast enough that a remote result prints without\n   waiting for a keystroke.\"\n  25)\n\n\n(def default-cadence\n  \"The tick owner's idle curve.  `tick-millis` is the base interval, what a\n   wake resets to and what a pending write holds, and an idle composition\n   backs off doubling to 200 ms.  Local input and the explicit stop trigger\n   nudge the wake source, so operator actions never wait the curve out; a\n   lost nudge costs at most the armed interval.\"\n  {:poll-ms tick-millis\n   :backoff {:factor 2 :ceiling-ms 200}})\n\n\n(def prompt \"yin> \")\n\n\n(def telemetry-text\n  (str \"--telemetry and --telemetry-stream are not part of the DaoStream \"\n       \"REPL slice: the telemetry emit path is built (yin.vm.telemetry, opt-in \"\n       \"via the :telemetry {:stream ...} construction option (\"\n       \"yin.vm.telemetry.implementation-plan.md), but composing a sink into \"\n       \"this shell is not, so the flags are rejected rather than ignored\"))\n\n\n(defn- parse-int\n  \"The decimal integer `text` names, or nil: total on every host.  At\n   most 15 digits, so the value is exact on every host (below 2^53) and\n   no host parser can overflow; a longer number is nil, which each flag\n   refuses as data.\"\n  [text]\n  (when (and (string? text) (re-matches #\"-?\\d{1,15}\" text))\n    #?(:cljd (int/parse text)\n       :cljs (js/parseInt text 10)\n       :clj (Long/parseLong text))))\n\n\n(defn parse-peer\n  \"A `--dht-peer` value, `host:port` or `[v6-host]:port`, as the DHT's\n   bootstrap contact `{:host h :port p}`.  The host must be an IP\n   literal: the DHT resolves no names.\"\n  [text]\n  (let [[_ v6 v4 port] (re-matches #\"(?:\\[([^\\]]+)\\]|([^:\\[\\]]+)):(\\d+)\"\n                                   (str text))\n        host (or v6 v4)\n        port (parse-int port)]\n    (cond\n      (not (datagram/valid-port? port))\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-peer takes host:port with a port from 1 \"\n                           \"to 65535, not \" (pr-str text))\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      (not (datagram/ip-literal? host))\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-peer host must be an IP literal, not \"\n                           (pr-str host))\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      :else {:host host :port port})))\n\n\n(defn- parse-manifest\n  \"A `--dht-manifest` value (the address as printed, with or without its\n   leading colon) as the segment address keyword.\"\n  [text]\n  (when (string? text)\n    (keyword (if (str/starts-with? text \":\") (subs text 1) text))))\n\n\n(def ^:private dht-flags\n  #{\"--dht-peer\" \"--dht-publish\" \"--dht-bind\" \"--dht-port\"\n    \"--dht-max-inbound-bytes\" \"--dht-manifest\" \"--dht-key\" \"--dht-principal\"\n    \"--dht-trust\" \"--dht-follow\"})\n\n\n(def max-follow\n  \"The most principals one node follows (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 7).\"\n  64)\n\n\n(defn parse-follow\n  \"A `--dht-follow` value, `<64 hex>@<host:port>`, as `{:principal hex\n   :host h :port p}`: the principal whose published head is followed, at\n   the one address its board is dialed at.  The host must be a loopback\n   IP literal (`localhost` is 127.0.0.1): following off loopback is\n   not in this release (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.1, 8.3).\"\n  [text]\n  (let [[_ principal v6 v4 port]\n        (re-matches #\"(?:ed25519:)?([0-9a-f]{64})@(?:\\[([^\\]]+)\\]|([^:\\[\\]@]+)):(\\d+)\"\n                    (str/replace-first (str text) #\"@localhost:\" \"@127.0.0.1:\"))\n        host (or v6 v4)\n        port (parse-int port)]\n    (cond\n      (nil? principal)\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow takes <64 lowercase hex>@<host:port>,\"\n                           \" not \" (pr-str text))\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      (not (datagram/valid-port? port))\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow takes a port from 1 to 65535, not \"\n                           (pr-str text))\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      (not (remote-channel/loopback-literal? host))\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow \" (pr-str text) \": a head board is \"\n                           \"followed on loopback only (127.0.0.1 or ::1)\")\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      :else {:principal principal :host host :port port})))\n\n\n(defn parse-principal\n  \"A `--dht-principal` value: a declared publisher's public key, exactly\n   64 lowercase hexadecimal characters (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.2).\"\n  [text]\n  (if (and (string? text) (re-matches #\"^[0-9a-f]{64}$\" text))\n    text\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-principal takes a public key of 64 lowercase \"\n                         \"hexadecimal characters, not \" (pr-str text))\n                    {:value text}))))\n\n\n(defn parse-trust\n  \"Parse the shared CLI/session trust configuration principal.\"\n  [text]\n  (state/parse-trust text))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The publisher's key file (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.5)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- split-path\n  \"`[dir name]` of a file path.\"\n  [path]\n  (if-let [i (str/last-index-of path \"/\")]\n    [(subs path 0 i) (subs path (inc i))]\n    [\".\" path]))\n\n\n(defn load-key\n  \"The publisher key `{:seed :public}` the key file at `path` holds, or a\n   refusal of startup with the reason: a file that does not exist, or one\n   `yin.vm.linker.sign/key-from-text` refuses.  Nothing falls back to a\n   fresh key, and no refusal carries the file's content.\"\n  [path]\n  (let [[dir name] (split-path path)\n        text (fs/read-file-text dir name)\n        key (when text (sign/key-from-text text))]\n    (cond\n      (nil? text)\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-key \" path \": the key file does not exist; \"\n                           \"nothing falls back to a fresh key (create one with \"\n                           \"--dht-keygen \" path \")\")\n                      {:path path}))\n\n      (= :refused (:status key))\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-key \" path \": the key file is refused (\"\n                           (:reason key) \")\")\n                      {:path path :reason (:reason key)}))\n\n      :else key)))\n\n\n(defn- ensure-parent!\n  \"Create the missing directories above `path`.\"\n  [path]\n  (let [[dir _] (split-path path)]\n    #?(:cljd (.createSync (io/Directory. dir) .recursive true)\n       :clj (.mkdirs (java.io.File. ^String dir))\n       :cljs (.mkdirSync (js/require \"fs\") dir #js {:recursive true}))))\n\n\n(defn- write-new-file!\n  \"Create `path` holding `text`, readable by its owner only where the host\n   can set that, refusing an existing file: a key file is never\n   overwritten.  The directories above it are created.\"\n  [path text]\n  (ensure-parent! path)\n  (let [exists (fn []\n                 (ex-info (str \"--dht-keygen \" path \": the file exists; \"\n                               \"a key file is never overwritten\")\n                          {:path path}))]\n    #?(:cljd (let [f (io/File. path)]\n               (when (.existsSync f) (throw (exists)))\n               (try (.createSync f .exclusive true)\n                    (catch Object _ (throw (exists))))\n               (.writeAsStringSync f text))\n       ;; O_CREAT | O_EXCL at 0600 in one call: owner-only from creation,\n       ;; never an existing file; written whole, fsynced, closed checked\n       :jolt (when (= :exists (posix-file/write-new-private! path (text/utf8-encode text)))\n               (throw (exists)))\n       :clj (let [p (.toPath (java.io.File. ^String path))\n                  owner-only\n                  (java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermissions/asFileAttribute\n                    (java.nio.file.attribute.PosixFilePermissions/fromString\n                      \"rw-------\"))]\n              (try\n                (try (java.nio.file.Files/createFile\n                       p (into-array java.nio.file.attribute.FileAttribute\n                                     [owner-only]))\n                     (catch UnsupportedOperationException _\n                       (java.nio.file.Files/createFile\n                         p (make-array java.nio.file.attribute.FileAttribute\n                                       0))))\n                (catch java.nio.file.FileAlreadyExistsException _\n                  (throw (exists))))\n              (spit path text))\n       :cljs (let [fs-module (js/require \"fs\")]\n               (try (.writeFileSync fs-module path text #js {:flag \"wx\" :mode\n                                                             384})\n                    (catch :default e\n                      (if (= \"EEXIST\" (.-code e))\n                        (throw (exists))\n                        (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-keygen \" path \": \"\n                                             (.-message e))\n                                        {:path path})))))))))\n\n\n(defn keygen!\n  \"Write a new key, from the host CSPRNG, to a new key file at `path`\n   (`--dht-keygen`), and answer the lines to print and the exit status.\n   The seed is never printed.\"\n  [path]\n  (let [key (sign/generate)]\n    (write-new-file! path (str (sign/key-text key) \"\\n\"))\n    {:lines (cond-> [(str \"dht: wrote a new Ed25519 key to \" path\n                          \"; its principal is \"\n                          (sign/principal (:public key))\n                          \". Keep the file: a lost key \"\n                          \"can never sign again, and a new key is a new \"\n                          \"principal.\")]\n              ;; dart:io sets no file permissions (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.5)\n              #?(:cljd true :default false)\n              (conj (str \"dht: WARNING: this host cannot restrict the k\"\n                         \"ey file's \"\n                         \"permissions; it is readable as the process um\"\n                         \"ask allows. \"\n                         \"Restrict it now: chmod 600 \" path)))\n     :exit 0}))\n\n\n(defn- dht-spec\n  \"Fold the `--dht-*` flags into the `dht:<dir>` spec, checked by\n   `yin.repl.store/checked-spec`.  They mean nothing to any other store,\n   and a bind address means nothing to a solo node, so each such use is\n   refused rather than ignored.\"\n  [spec {:keys [flags peers publish? bind-host bind-port max-inbound-bytes\n                manifest follow principals]}]\n  (cond\n    (and (seq flags) (not= :dht (:type spec)))\n    (throw (ex-info (str (str/join \", \" (sort flags))\n                         \" need --index-store dht:<dir>\")\n                    {:flags (vec (sort flags))}))\n\n    (not= :dht (:type spec)) spec\n\n    (and (empty? peers) (or bind-host bind-port))\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-bind and --dht-port need a --dht-peer: \"\n                         \"with no peers the DHT store is solo and opens \"\n                         \"no socket\")\n                    {}))\n\n    (and bind-host (not (datagram/ip-literal? bind-host)))\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-bind must be an IP literal, not \"\n                         (pr-str bind-host))\n                    {:value bind-host}))\n\n    (and (seq follow) (empty? peers))\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow needs a --dht-peer: a followed head's \"\n                         \"index is fetched over the DHT\")\n                    {}))\n\n    (some #(not (contains? (set principals) (:principal %))) follow)\n    (let [p (:principal (first (remove #(contains? (set principals)\n                                                   (:principal %))\n                                       follow)))]\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--dht-follow \" p \" follows a principal that is not\"\n                           \" declared: add --dht-principal \" p)\n                      {:principal p})))\n\n    (not= (count follow) (count (distinct (map :principal follow))))\n    (throw (ex-info \"--dht-follow names each principal once: one source each\"\n                    {}))\n\n    (> (count follow) max-follow)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"at most \" max-follow \" principals may be followed\")\n                    {:count (count follow)}))\n\n    :else\n    (store/checked-spec\n      (cond-> (assoc spec :peers (vec peers) :publish? (boolean publish?))\n        bind-host (assoc :bind-host bind-host)\n        bind-port (assoc :bind-port bind-port)\n        max-inbound-bytes (assoc :max-inbound-bytes max-inbound-bytes)\n        manifest (assoc :manifest manifest)\n        ;; a pin wins for the run: it follows nothing (5.6)\n        (and (seq follow) (not manifest)) (assoc :follow (vec follow))\n        (and (seq follow) manifest) (assoc :follow-suspended true)))))\n\n\n(defn- dht-number\n  [flag text]\n  (or (parse-int text)\n      (throw (ex-info (str flag \" takes an integer of at most 15 digits, not \"\n                           (pr-str text))\n                      {:value text}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The subcommands: `keygen` and `dht init|serve|join`\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- home-dir*\n  \"The user's home directory, or nil where the host has none (ClojureDart).\"\n  []\n  #?(:cljd nil\n     :clj (System/getProperty \"user.home\")\n     :cljs (some-> js/process .-env .-HOME)))\n\n\n(defn- home-dir\n  []\n  (or (home-dir*)\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"no home directory on this host: give --dir and \"\n                           \"--key explicitly\")\n                      {}))))\n\n\n(defn- state-path\n  \"`~/.yin/<name><suffix>`.\"\n  [name suffix]\n  (str (home-dir) \"/.yin/\" name suffix))\n\n\n(defn- peer-text\n  \"A peer as the DHT takes it: `localhost` is the loopback IP literal.\"\n  [text]\n  (str/replace-first (str text) #\"^localhost:\" \"127.0.0.1:\"))\n\n\n(defn parse-token\n  \"A join token `yin:<host:port>/<principal>` as `[peer principal]` flag\n   values; the principal accepts an `ed25519:` prefix.  A token with a\n   third part is refused: a manifest is a pin and belongs to\n   `--dht-manifest` (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.8).\"\n  [text]\n  (let [[_ peer principal more]\n        (re-matches #\"yin:([^/]+)/(?:ed25519:)?([^/]+)(/.*)?\" (str text))]\n    (cond\n      (and peer more)\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"a join token has two parts, yin:<host:port>/\"\n                           \"<principal>; \" (pr-str text) \" has a third: a \"\n                           \"manifest is a pin and belongs to --dht-manifest\")\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      (not peer)\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"a join token looks like yin:<host:port>/\"\n                           \"<principal>, as `dht: join token:` prints it, \"\n                           \"not \" (pr-str text))\n                      {:value text}))\n\n      :else [peer principal])))\n\n\n(defn- keygen-args\n  [args]\n  (loop [args (seq args) name nil file nil]\n    (if-let [arg (first args)]\n      (if (= \"--name\" arg)\n        (recur (nnext args) (second args) file)\n        (recur (next args) name arg))\n      [\"--dht-keygen\" (or file (state-path (or name \"publisher\") \".key\"))])))\n\n\n(defn- dht-args\n  \"The legacy flags of `dht init|serve|join`.  `init` publishes (its key is\n   made on first use); `serve` stores for others; `join <token>` reads,\n   declaring and following the token's principal at its address.\n   Every node needs a `--peer` to open a socket (the two-node start in\n   yin.repl.md); `--listen [ip:]port` is the node's own address.  Returns\n   `[args {:new-key path}]`.\"\n  [[verb & args]]\n  (when-not (#{\"init\" \"serve\" \"join\"} verb)\n    (throw (ex-info \"usage: yin-repl dht init|serve|join [token] [options]\"\n                    {})))\n  (loop [args (seq args)\n         o {:name \"node\" :peers [] :rest []}]\n    (if-let [arg (first args)]\n      (let [v (second args)]\n        (case arg\n          \"--name\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :name v))\n          \"--dir\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :dir v))\n          \"--key\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :key v))\n          \"--peer\" (recur (nnext args) (update o :peers conj (peer-text v)))\n          \"--listen\" (recur (nnext args) (assoc o :listen v))\n          (if (and (= \"join\" verb) (not (:token o))\n                   (not (str/starts-with? arg \"-\")))\n            (recur (next args) (assoc o :token arg))\n            (recur (next args) (update o :rest conj arg)))))\n      (let [{:keys [name dir key peers listen token rest]} o\n            [tpeer principal] (when (= \"join\" verb)\n                                (parse-token token))\n            tpeer (some-> tpeer peer-text)\n            peers (cond-> peers tpeer (conj tpeer))\n            [_ lhost lport] (when listen\n                              (re-matches #\"(?:(.+):)?(\\d+)\" listen))\n            key (or key (when (= \"init\" verb) (state-path name \".key\")))]\n        [(cond-> [\"--index-store\" (str \"dht:\" (or dir (state-path name \"\")))]\n           (= \"init\" verb) (conj \"--dht-publish\")\n           key (into [\"--dht-key\" key])\n           lhost (into [\"--dht-bind\" lhost])\n           lport (into [\"--dht-port\" lport])\n           ;; join follows the principal's head at the token's address and\n           ;; passes no manifest: its first manifest is the first head it\n           ;; installs (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.8)\n           principal (into [\"--dht-principal\" principal\n                            \"--dht-follow\" (str principal \"@\" tpeer)])\n           true (into (mapcat #(vector \"--dht-peer\" %)) peers)\n           true (into rest))\n         {:new-key (when (= \"init\" verb) key)\n          :name name\n          :dir (or dir (state-path name \"\"))\n          ;; only `init` publishes and signs by default: a saved --dht-publish\n          ;; or --dht-key must not survive `serve` or `join` (explicit flags\n          ;; named on that command line still stand)\n          :unset (when-not (= \"init\" verb) #{\"--dht-publish\" \"--dht-key\"})}]))))\n\n\n(defn- plain-args\n  \"The plain form's `--name n` and `--dir d`, which name the node directory\n   its state file lives in (default `~/.yin/node`), removed from `args`.\n   Answers `[args extra]`.\"\n  [args]\n  (loop [args (seq args) rest [] node-name \"node\" dir nil]\n    (if-let [arg (first args)]\n      (case arg\n        \"--name\" (if (next args)\n                   (recur (nnext args) rest (second args) dir)\n                   (recur (next args) (conj rest arg) node-name dir))\n        \"--dir\" (if (next args)\n                  (recur (nnext args) rest node-name (second args))\n                  (recur (next args) (conj rest arg) node-name dir))\n        (recur (next args) (conj rest arg) node-name dir))\n      [rest {:name node-name :dir dir}])))\n\n\n(defn expand-args\n  \"Turn the subcommand surface into the flags `parse-args` reads:\n   `keygen [--name n | file]` and `dht init|serve|join`.  Anything else\n   passes through unchanged.  Answers `[args extra]`, `extra` carrying what\n   the arguments say beyond flags: `:new-key` (a key file `init` makes),\n   `:name` and `:dir` (the node directory), and `:unset` (saved flags the\n   subcommand clears).\"\n  [args]\n  (case (first args)\n    \"keygen\" [(keygen-args (rest args)) nil]\n    \"dht\" (dht-args (rest args))\n    (plain-args args)))\n\n\n(defn- parse-vm\n  \"A `--vm` value as the evaluator keyword the shell takes.\"\n  [text]\n  (let [vm-type (some-> text keyword)]\n    (if (contains? shell/vm-labels vm-type)\n      vm-type\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"--vm takes one of \"\n                           (str/join \", \" (map name (sort (keys shell/vm-labels))))\n                           \", not \" (pr-str text))\n                      {:value text})))))\n\n\n(defn- parse-codec\n  \"A `--codec` value, `cbor` or `transit`, as the keyword `connect/codec-profiles`\n   names.\"\n  [value]\n  (let [k (some-> value keyword)]\n    (if (contains? connect/codec-profiles k)\n      k\n      (throw (ex-info (str #?(:cljd \"--codec takes cbor or transit, not \"\n                              :jolt \"--codec takes cbor, not \"\n                              :default \"--codec takes cbor or transit, not \") (pr-str value))\n                      {:value value})))))\n\n\n(defn parse-args\n  \"Parse the host arguments.  `--telemetry` and `--telemetry-stream` are\n   rejected rather than ignored.  `--index-store` is parsed into\n   `:index-store-spec`: `:mem` (also what omission means),\n   `{:type :file :dir dir}`, or the DHT store `{:type :dht :dir dir ...}`;\n   and a missing value, an unknown scheme, or an empty directory is\n   refused here, by `yin.repl.store/parse-arg`, before any host composes.\n\n   The DHT store's options are their own flags (yin.repl.dht):\n   `--dht-peer host:port`, repeatable, the bootstrap contacts; none\n   means solo, with no socket; `--dht-publish`, the separate declaration\n   that shares the store; `--dht-bind ip` and `--dht-port p`, the\n   socket's address, loopback and ephemeral unless given;\n   `--dht-max-inbound-bytes n`, the inbound storage bound; and\n   `--dht-manifest address`, a remote index to hydrate before the first\n   evaluation; `--dht-key file`, the publisher's stable key file\n   (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.5); `--dht-principal hex`, repeatable, a\n   publisher whose signed names this node honors (7.1);\n   `--dht-trust ed25519:hex`, repeatable, a principal whose modules the\n   session's admission interpreter admits (7.5), apart from the\n   declarations; and\n   `--dht-follow hex@host:port`, repeatable, a declared publisher whose\n   published head this node follows at that loopback address\n   (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.5, 5.8).  Any of them\n   without `dht:<dir>` is refused.  `--dht-keygen file` writes a new key\n   file and exits.\"\n  [args]\n  (loop [args (seq args)\n         opts {:headless? false :host nil :port nil :rejected []\n               :index-store-spec :mem}\n         dht {:flags #{} :peers []}]\n    (if-let [arg (first args)]\n      (let [dht (cond-> dht (dht-flags arg) (update :flags conj arg))\n            value (second args)]\n        (case arg\n          \"--port\" (recur (nnext args)\n                          (assoc opts :port (when-some [p (second args)]\n                                              (let [n (parse-int p)]\n                                                (if (and n (<= 0 n 65535))\n                                                  n\n                                                  (throw (ex-info\n                                                           (str \"--port takes a \"\n                                                                \"port from 0 to \"\n                                                                \"65535, not \"\n                                                                (pr-str p))\n                                                           {:value p}))))))\n                          dht)\n          \"--host\" (throw (ex-info (str \"--host is gone: --port serves on all \"\n                                        \"interfaces, so it answers on \"\n                                        \"localhost and on this machine's IP\")\n                                   {}))\n          \"--codec\" (recur (nnext args)\n                           (assoc opts :codec (parse-codec value))\n                           dht)\n          \"--headless\" (recur (next args) (assoc opts :headless? true) dht)\n          \"--vm\" (recur (nnext args)\n                        (assoc opts :vm-type (parse-vm (second args)))\n                        dht)\n          \"--index-store\" (recur (nnext args)\n                                 (assoc opts\n                                        :index-store-spec\n                                        (store/parse-arg (second args)))\n                                 dht)\n          \"--dht-peer\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n                              (update dht :peers conj (parse-peer value)))\n          \"--dht-publish\" (recur (next args) opts (assoc dht :publish? true))\n          \"--dht-bind\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n                              (assoc dht :bind-host (str value)))\n          \"--dht-port\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n                              (assoc dht :bind-port (dht-number arg value)))\n          \"--dht-max-inbound-bytes\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n                                           (assoc dht :max-inbound-bytes\n                                                  (dht-number arg value)))\n          \"--dht-manifest\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n                                  (assoc dht :manifest (parse-manifest value)))\n          \"--dht-key\" (recur (nnext args)\n                             (assoc opts :dht-key-file\n                                    (or value\n                                        (throw (ex-info\n                                                 \"--dht-key needs a key file\"\n                                                 {}))))\n                             dht)\n          \"--dht-principal\" (recur (nnext args)\n                                   (update opts :principals (fnil conj [])\n                                           (parse-principal value))\n                                   dht)\n          \"--dht-trust\" (recur (nnext args)\n                               (update-in opts [:admission :trusted]\n                                          (fnil conj #{}) (parse-trust value))\n                               dht)\n          \"--dht-follow\" (recur (nnext args) opts\n                                (update dht :follow (fnil conj [])\n                                        (parse-follow value)))\n          \"--dht-keygen\" (recur (nnext args)\n                                (assoc opts :dht-keygen\n                                       (or value\n                                           (throw (ex-info\n                                                    \"--dht-keygen needs a file\"\n                                                    {}))))\n                                dht)\n          \"--telemetry\" (recur (next args) (update opts :rejected conj arg) dht)\n          \"--telemetry-stream\" (recur (nnext args)\n                                      (update opts :rejected conj arg) dht)\n          (recur (next args) (update opts :extra (fnil conj []) arg) dht)))\n      (update opts :index-store-spec dht-spec\n              (assoc dht :principals (:principals opts))))))\n\n\n(defn boot\n  \"Create the composition: one shell, one input medium, one cursor held only by\n   the step owner, and the host WebSocket adapter `(connect ...)` attaches\n   through.  The parsed `:index-store-spec` reaches the shell's store\n   selection, which resolves and opens it once at construction.  A custody\n   composition (:custody, yin.vm.ucf.compose) rides the shell as data; the\n   step owner below is the only thing that steps it.\n\n   `:admission` is the session's admission policy (yin.vm.linker.dht.md\n   7.5; `--dht-trust` sets its `:trusted`), by default `{}`: the key's\n   own principal only.  It is apart from `:principals`: a declared\n   publisher is not a trusted one.\"\n  ([] (boot {}))\n  ([opts] (driver/create-state\n            {:host (or (:adapter opts) (host/websocket))\n             :repl (repl.frontends/create-state\n                     (cond-> {:index-store-spec (:index-store-spec opts)\n                              :dht-key (:dht-key opts)\n                              :principals (:principals opts)\n                              :admission (:admission opts {})\n                              :ws-host (:ws-host opts)\n                              :write-heads! (:write-heads! opts)\n                              :custody (:custody opts)}\n                       (:vm-type opts) (assoc :vm-type (:vm-type opts))))})))\n\n\n(def bind-all-host\n  \"`--port` listens on every interface: loopback and the machine's own IP.\"\n  \"0.0.0.0\")\n\n\n(defn pick-ip\n  \"The address to advertise among a machine's non-loopback IPv4 addresses:\n   the first private-network one (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), since a\n   tunnel or VPN interface can list ahead of the LAN's; else the first;\n   else loopback.\"\n  [ips]\n  (or (first (filter #(re-matches #\"10\\..*|192\\.168\\..*|172\\.(1[6-9]|2\\d|3[01])\\..*\"\n                                  %)\n                     ips))\n      (first ips)\n      \"127.0.0.1\"))\n\n\n(defn local-ip\n  \"This machine's address for `--port`'s banner and advertised host.\n   ClojureDart can only list interfaces asynchronously, so it advertises\n   loopback.\"\n  []\n  (pick-ip\n    #?(:cljd nil\n       :clj (try (doall\n                   (for [^java.net.NetworkInterface ni\n                         (enumeration-seq\n                           (java.net.NetworkInterface/getNetworkInterfaces))\n                         :when (and (.isUp ni) (not (.isLoopback ni)))\n                         ^java.net.InetAddress a (enumeration-seq\n                                                   (.getInetAddresses ni))\n                         :when (instance? java.net.Inet4Address a)]\n                     (.getHostAddress a)))\n                 (catch Exception _ nil))\n       :cljs (try (doall\n                    (for [addrs (array-seq (js/Object.values\n                                             (.networkInterfaces\n                                               (js/require \"os\"))))\n                          ^js a (array-seq addrs)\n                          :when (and (contains? #{\"IPv4\" 4} (.-family a))\n                                     (not (.-internal a)))]\n                      (.-address a)))\n                  (catch :default _ nil)))))\n\n\n(defn boot-server\n  \"Compose the served endpoint for `--port`, or nil when no port was asked for.\n\n   `serve!` returns immediately.  Whether a listener bound is a fact the\n   endpoint's lifecycle medium reports, and the ticker prints it.\"\n  [opts]\n  (when (:port opts)\n    (serve/serve! {:bind-port (:port opts)\n                   :bind-host bind-all-host\n                   :advertised-host (local-ip)\n                   :host (or (:adapter opts) (host/websocket))\n                   :codec (get connect/codec-profiles (:codec opts :cbor))})))\n\n\n(def help-lines\n  \"What `--help` prints: every subcommand and flag `parse-args` and\n   `expand-args` read.\"\n  [\"yin-repl: the interface to datom.world\"\n   \"\"\n   \"usage: yin-repl [flags]\"\n   \"       yin-repl dht init|serve|join [token] [options]\"\n   \"       yin-repl keygen [--name n | file]\"\n   \"\"\n   \"subcommands:\"\n   \"  dht init          publish: share the store, make the key on first run\"\n   \"  dht serve         storing peer: fetch only, no key\"\n   \"  dht join <token>  reader: follow the head of the token's principal;\"\n   \"                    the token is yin:<host:port>/<principal>\"\n   \"  keygen            write a new Ed25519 key file and exit\"\n   \"\"\n   \"dht options:\"\n   \"  --name n          state under ~/.yin/<n> (default: node)\"\n   \"  --dir d           state directory, instead of ~/.yin/<name>\"\n   \"  --key file        key file (init: default ~/.yin/<name>.key)\"\n   \"  --listen [ip:]port  this node's DHT socket\"\n   \"  --peer host:port  a peer to contact; repeatable; localhost or an IP\"\n   \"\"\n   \"flags:\"\n   \"  --port n          serve this shell to other shells over WebSockets, on\"\n   \"                    all interfaces: localhost and this machine's IP\"\n   \"  --codec c         wire codec served: cbor (default) or transit\"\n   \"  --headless        no prompt, endpoint only; needs --port\"\n   \"  --index-store s   mem (default), file:<dir> or dht:<dir>\"\n   \"  --vm type         ast-walker, semantic (default), stack or register\"\n   \"  --name n, --dir d the node directory holding the saved state\"\n   \"                    (default ~/.yin/node)\"\n   \"  --reset           forget the saved state; start from the command line\"\n   \"  --no-state        neither read nor write the saved state\"\n   \"  --help, -h        print this and exit\"\n   \"\"\n   \"saved state: the flags a node starts with are kept in <node dir>/state.edn,\"\n   \"and a bare `yin-repl` starts that node again. Flags change it: a repeated\"\n   \"flag (--dht-peer, --dht-principal, --dht-trust, --dht-follow) replaces\"\n   \"its saved values, and the result is saved. --dht-manifest and\"\n   \"--dht-keygen are never saved. A followed head is kept in\"\n   \"<dht dir>/heads.edn.\"\n   \"\"\n   \"dht flags (need --index-store dht:<dir>, except --dht-keygen):\"\n   \"  --dht-peer host:port        bootstrap contact (IP literal); repeatable\"\n   \"  --dht-publish               share the store's content with peers\"\n   \"  --dht-bind ip               socket address; needs a peer\"\n   \"  --dht-port p                socket port; needs a peer\"\n   \"  --dht-max-inbound-bytes n   inbound payload bound (default 64 MiB)\"\n   \"  --dht-manifest :segment/... remote index to hydrate first; needs a peer;\"\n   \"                              a pin: this run follows nothing\"\n   \"  --dht-key file              the publisher's key file\"\n   \"  --dht-principal hex         a publisher whose signed names to honor;\"\n   \"                              repeatable\"\n   \"  --dht-trust ed25519:hex     a principal whose modules to admit (not\"\n   \"                              implied by --dht-principal); repeatable\"\n   \"  (trust p), (untrust p), (trusted) edit/show session trust; reset\"\n   \"                              restores the configured --dht-trust set\"\n   \"  --dht-follow hex@host:port  follow a declared publisher's head at its\"\n   \"                              loopback address; repeatable\"\n   \"  --dht-keygen file           write a new key file and exit\"\n   \"\"\n   \"--telemetry and --telemetry-stream are rejected.\"\n   \"Unrecognized arguments are ignored.\"\n   \"See src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md for the walk-through.\"])\n\n\n(defn- save-state!\n  \"Save the flags this run resolved to the node directory's state file when\n   they differ from what was saved (nothing is written for a bare run with\n   nothing saved), and answer the banner's lines about it.  A file that\n   cannot be written is a warning, never a refusal: the node still starts.\"\n  [dir saved resolved reset?]\n  (let [path (state/path dir)\n        changed (state/changed (or saved {}) resolved)]\n    (try\n      (cond\n        (and (nil? saved) (empty? resolved) (not reset?))\n        []\n\n        (nil? saved)\n        (do (state/save! dir resolved)\n            [(str \"state: \" (if reset? \"reset, \" \"\") \"saved to \" path\n                  \"; a bare yin-repl starts this node again\")])\n\n        (seq changed)\n        (do (state/save! dir resolved)\n            [(str \"state: resumed from \" path \" (\" (state/describe saved)\n                  \"); the command line changed \" (str/join \", \" changed)\n                  \"; saved\")])\n\n        :else\n        [(str \"state: resumed from \" path \" (\" (state/describe resolved) \")\")])\n      (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                :clj Throwable\n                :cljs :default)\n             e\n        [(str \"state: WARNING: could not save \" path \": \"\n              (or (ex-message e) (str e)))]))))\n\n\n(defn startup\n  \"Parse the arguments and compose the whole shell (the store the parsed\n   `:index-store-spec` names included), or answer the refusal text.  This\n   is the one gate every host's `-main` passes through before it prints a\n   banner or starts a loop, so an invalid `--index-store`, an unsupported\n   host for it, or a directory that cannot be opened refuses startup with\n   its reason rather than composing a shell around a fallback store.\n\n   Only a designed refusal is answered: an error carrying no ex-info\n   keeps its stack trace, because it is a defect, not an operator error.\n\n   `--dht-keygen` composes nothing: it writes the key file and answers\n   `{:lines [...] :exit 0}` for the host to print and exit with.  A\n   `--dht-key` file is loaded here (`load-key`); the key reaches the\n   shell, and only its principal reaches the options the banner reads.\n   An `:adapter` option is the host WebSocket adapter the shell, the head\n   board and the endpoint compose over (else `host/websocket`).\"\n  ([args] (startup args nil))\n  ([args {:keys [persist? home adapter]}]\n   (try\n     (if (some #{\"--help\" \"-h\"} args)\n       {:lines help-lines :exit 0}\n       (let [reset? (boolean (some #{\"--reset\"} args))\n             no-state? (boolean (some #{\"--no-state\"} args))\n             [args {:keys [new-key unset] node-name :name dir :dir}]\n             (expand-args (remove #{\"--reset\" \"--no-state\"} args))\n             node-dir (when (and persist? (not no-state?)\n                                 (not (some #{\"--dht-keygen\"} args)))\n                        (or dir\n                            (some-> (or home (home-dir*))\n                                    (str \"/.yin/\" node-name))))\n             saved (when (and node-dir (not reset?)) (state/load-flags node-dir))\n             {:keys [flags rest]} (state/split-args args)\n             resolved (state/resolve-flags saved flags unset)\n             args (into (state/flags->args resolved) rest)\n             made (when (and new-key (nil? (fs/read-file-text\n                                             (first (split-path new-key))\n                                             (second (split-path new-key)))))\n                    (:lines (keygen! new-key)))\n             opts (cond-> (try (parse-args args)\n                               (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                                         :clj Throwable\n                                         :cljs :default)\n                                      e\n                                 (throw (if (and saved (ex-data e))\n                                          (ex-info (str (ex-message e)\n                                                        \" (with the saved state in \"\n                                                        (state/path node-dir)\n                                                        \"; --reset forgets it)\")\n                                                   (ex-data e))\n                                          e))))\n                    made (assoc :startup-lines made)\n                    adapter (assoc :adapter adapter))]\n         (if-some [path (:dht-keygen opts)]\n           (keygen! path)\n           (let [key (some-> (:dht-key-file opts) load-key)\n                 opts (cond-> opts\n                        key (assoc :publisher (sign/principal (:public key))))\n                 ;; the head board is served and dialed over the host's\n                 ;; WebSocket seam (yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.1)\n                 state' (boot (assoc opts\n                                     :dht-key key\n                                     :ws-host (or (:adapter opts)\n                                                  (host/websocket))))\n                 server (boot-server opts)\n                 lines (into (vec (repl.dht/follow-lines (:repl state')))\n                             (when node-dir\n                               (save-state! node-dir saved resolved reset?)))]\n             {:opts (cond-> opts\n                      (seq lines) (update :startup-lines\n                                          #(into (vec %) lines)))\n              :state state'\n              :server server}))))\n     (catch #?(:cljd Object\n               :clj Throwable\n               :cljs :default)\n            e\n       (if (ex-data e)\n         {:refusal (or (ex-message e) (str e))}\n         (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  \"Print why the composition refused to start and end the process with a\n   failing status, so a refused startup never looks like a working shell.\"\n  [refusal]\n  (println refusal)\n  #?(:cljd (io/exit 1)\n     :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit 1))\n     :clj (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 1)\n     :cljs (.exit js/process 1)))\n\n\n(defn- custody-of\n  [state]\n  (get-in state [:repl :custody]))\n\n\n(defn close-index-store!\n  \"Release the composition's lifecycle resources before the host exits:\n   the custody composition's journals first (compose/close! -- the\n   authority and every journal backend the composition opened; it never\n   throws, so a failed close never blocks the exit), then the index\n   store's own resources (in durable mode, the exclusive directory\n   lock).  The shell has already stopped when a host calls this; the\n   memory store has nothing to release.  A DHT store's head board and\n   dials have been asked to stop and drained by then (`stop-tick`);\n   `yin.repl.dht/close!` is the last resort for whatever the budget\n   left.\"\n  [state]\n  (when-some [c (custody-of state)]\n    (compose/close! c))\n  (repl.dht/close! (:repl state))\n  (store/close! (get-in state [:repl :index-store])))\n\n\n(defn- entry-text\n  [entry]\n  (or (get entry driver/text-key) (get entry serve/text-key)))\n\n\n(defn banner\n  \"Text the composition prints before the first prompt, given parsed\n   options.  A DHT store states here (before its node steps once, so\n   before anything is shared) what it will share: the whole store when\n   `--dht-publish` is given, nothing otherwise (yin.repl.dht/banner).\"\n  [opts]\n  (let [spec (:index-store-spec opts)\n        dht? (= :dht (:type spec))]\n    (cond-> (vec (:startup-lines opts))\n      (seq (:rejected opts)) (conj telemetry-text)\n      (and (:port opts) (zero? (:port opts)))\n      (conj (str \"serving on all interfaces on an ephemeral port; the Serving\"\n                 \" line names it once bound. Anyone who can reach this port\"\n                 \" can evaluate code in this shell; there is no\"\n                 \" authentication.\"))\n\n      (and (:port opts) (not (zero? (:port opts))))\n      (conj (str \"serving on all interfaces: ws://127.0.0.1:\" (:port opts)\n                 (let [ip (local-ip)]\n                   (if (= \"127.0.0.1\" ip)\n                     \" and ws://<this machine's IP>:\"\n                     (str \" and ws://\" ip \":\")))\n                 (:port opts)\n                 \". Anyone who can reach this port can evaluate code in this\"\n                 \" shell; there is no authentication.\"))\n      (and (:headless? opts) (not (:port opts)))\n      (conj \"--headless has nothing to attend without a served endpoint\")\n      dht? (into (repl.dht/banner spec))\n      (and dht? (:publish? spec))\n      (conj (str \"dht: the code index and the code itself are shared: every\"\n                 \" evaluated program's rows are written to the index store\"\n                 \" each round\"))\n      (and dht? (:publisher opts))\n      (conj (str \"dht: names published here are signed by principal \"\n                 (:publisher opts) \" (key from \" (:dht-key-file opts) \")\"))\n      (and dht? (not (:publisher opts)))\n      (conj (str \"dht: no --dht-key: (yin.link/publish ...) is refused; names\"\n                 \" are still read, resolved, loaded and linked\"))\n      #?(:cljd false :jolt true :default false)\n      (into [shell/host-text\n             ;; J5: the native parser is disclosed; with no usable library\n             ;; Python is left out of the catalog and the reason is printed\n             #?(:jolt (if (python.antlr.parser/available?)\n                        (str \"python: the native C++ ANTLR4 parser\"\n                             \" (native/jolt-parser), in process; no process\"\n                             \" crash isolation\")\n                        \"python: unavailable-parser (the native parser library is missing or incompatible; bb build:jolt-native)\")\n                :default nil)]))))\n\n\n(defn- exit-with!\n  \"Print a composition that ends at startup -- `--dht-keygen` -- and end\n   the process with its status.\"\n  [{:keys [lines exit]}]\n  (doseq [line lines]\n    (println line))\n  #?(:cljd (io/exit exit)\n     :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit exit))\n     :clj (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) (int exit))\n     :cljs (.exit js/process exit)))\n\n\n(defn- custody-control-step\n  \"The custody control plane's tick (r3 1.12): the renewals, pending\n   releases and request retries of every holder, the judge step and the\n   front steps of whatever custody composition the shell carries.  It\n   runs immediately after the DHT step and before the refusal and\n   admission branches, so custody progress and cleanup do not wait for\n   a hydration to finish and survive a refused store.  No program work\n   happens here whatever the shell's state.\"\n  [state]\n  (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n    (assoc-in state [:repl :custody] (compose/control-step c))\n    state))\n\n\n(defn- custody-program-step\n  \"The custody program step's tick: activation, execution and export\n   preparation under the split driver's own revalidated tenure.  It runs\n   after the shell's own step and before the endpoint's, and never once\n   the shell has stopped -- the stop latch is set in the shell's place.\"\n  [state]\n  (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n    (assoc-in state [:repl :custody] (compose/program-step c))\n    state))\n\n\n(defn- custody-stop\n  \"Latch the local stop on every holder of the shell's custody\n   composition (compose/stop): program work ends, control cleanup\n   continues through the bounded shutdown drain.  Idempotent, and a\n   no-op with no composition.\"\n  [state]\n  (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n    (assoc-in state [:repl :custody] (compose/stop c))\n    state))\n\n\n(defn- custody-owed?\n  \"True while the shell's custody composition owes a control-plane\n   write (compose/owed-control-write?): a pending write keeps the tick\n   owner's cadence at the base interval, like the driver's own.\"\n  [state]\n  (if-some [c (custody-of state)]\n    (compose/owed-control-write? c)\n    false))\n\n\n(defn step-all\n  \"One tick of the single step owner: the local shell first, then the served\n   endpoint, against the same shell value.  A DHT index store's node is stepped\n   first (yin.repl.dht/step): its lines print before the shell's, a\n   hydration still outstanding leaves every typed line waiting in the\n   input medium, and a refused one stops the shell.  The custody control\n   plane is stepped next, before the refusal and admission branches, so\n   renewals, pending releases and request retries continue during\n   hydration and past a refusal.  A `--port` process\n   serves one shared shell, as v1's atom made it: the driver evaluates this\n   tick's local\n   lines first, so a definition typed at the local prompt is already in the\n   shell the endpoint evaluates remote requests against in the same tick, and\n   the endpoint's shell (remote definitions included) is threaded back\n   before the next tick.  The custody program step follows the driver's own\n   step and precedes the endpoint's; once the shell has stopped it is\n   replaced by the stop latch, so no custody program call runs after\n   `:running?` turns false.  A require parked on a closure load the node\n   ended this tick is re-checked once, before any typed line, with no line\n   of its own (yin.repl/recheck-on-tick, yin.vm.linker.dht.md 8.2),\n   and linker progress resumes a parked run (7.5): its text prints after the\n   node's lines.  Returns `[state server lines]`;\n   the caller only prints.\"\n  [state server now]\n  (let [[repl dht-lines events] (repl.dht/step (:repl state) now)\n        state (custody-control-step (assoc state :repl repl))]\n    (cond\n      (repl.dht/refusal repl)\n      [(custody-stop (assoc state :running? false)) server dht-lines]\n\n      (not (repl.dht/admitting? repl))\n      [state server dht-lines]\n\n      :else\n      (let [[repl rechecked] (shell/recheck-on-tick (:repl state) events)\n            dht-lines (cond-> dht-lines\n                        (seq rechecked) (conj rechecked))\n            state (assoc state :repl repl)\n            stepped (driver/repl-step state now)\n            stepped (if (:running? stepped)\n                      (custody-program-step stepped)\n                      (custody-stop stepped))\n            [entries state'] (driver/take-outbox stepped)\n            server' (when server\n                      (serve/step (assoc-in server [:repl] (:repl state'))\n                                  now))\n            [server-entries server''] (if server'\n                                        (serve/take-outbox server')\n                                        [[] nil])\n            state'' (if server'' (assoc state' :repl (:repl server'')) state')]\n        [state'' server'' (into dht-lines\n                                (map entry-text)\n                                (into (vec entries) server-entries))]))))\n\n\n(defn moved?\n  \"The tick owner's cadence bit, computed from this tick's own results: true\n   when there are lines to print, when the endpoint reports movement (a\n   woken probe, a published notice), or when either composition still owes a\n   write -- the shell's own or a custody control-plane one.  A pending write\n   keeps cadence at the base interval; it must never wait out a backoff\n   ceiling.\"\n  [state server lines]\n  (boolean (or (seq lines)\n               (driver/pending-write? state)\n               (repl.dht/busy? (:repl state))\n               (custody-owed? state)\n               (and server (serve/moved? server)))))\n\n\n(defn prompt-owed?\n  \"True when the operator is owed a fresh prompt after a tick: the tick\n   printed lines, or it consumed input that printed nothing (a silent\n   assignment), which only the input cursor's advance reveals.\"\n  [state state' lines]\n  (boolean (or (seq lines)\n               (not= (:input-cursor state) (:input-cursor state')))))\n\n\n(defn exit-status\n  \"The process status a host exits with once the shell stops: 1 when the\n   DHT store refused after startup (a failed bind, a hydration that could\n   not complete), else 0.\"\n  [state]\n  (if (repl.dht/refusal (:repl state)) 1 0))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Shutdown\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def stop-ticks\n  \"How many ticks a host steps a stopping endpoint before exiting anyway; each\n   tick lasts at least `tick-millis`.  The\n   endpoint's `:stopped` fact comes from the host's own close completion, which\n   a wedged listener may never deposit; the shell says so and exits rather than\n   hanging on it.\"\n  200)\n\n\n(def stop-timeout-text\n  \";; the endpoint did not report :stopped; exiting anyway\")\n\n\n(def stop-join-millis\n  \"How long a host thread may wait for the step owner to finish shutdown.\n\n   The drain performs `stop-ticks + 1` steps with a sleep between them, so it\n   needs strictly more than `tick-millis x stop-ticks`; a join of exactly that\n   expires mid-drain and the announced timeout never prints.\"\n  (* tick-millis (+ stop-ticks 2)))\n\n\n(defn request-stop!\n  \"The explicit stop trigger: append the shell's own quit line to the input\n   medium.\n\n   This is the whole of what a host signal handler, a headless supervisor, or a\n   test does to stop the composition.  It is a line producer like any other, so\n   the one step owner still performs the shutdown: `(quit)` stops the shell,\n   and the shell stopping is what stops the endpoint.  The wake source, when\n   one is supplied, is nudged so the step owner runs the quit line at once\n   rather than at the idle interval it had armed.\"\n  ([state]\n   (driver/submit-line! (:input state) \"(quit)\"))\n  ([state w]\n   (let [result (request-stop! state)]\n     (when w (wake/nudge! w))\n     result)))\n\n\n(defn stop-tick\n  \"One shutdown tick for the shell's head board and the endpoint, both of\n   which have already been asked to stop (`yin.repl.dht/stop!`,\n   `serve/stop!`).  Returns `[state server lines stopped?]`, the node's\n   lines before the endpoint's.\n\n   `serve/stopped?` is the whole of the endpoint's exit condition, and\n   `yin.repl.dht/stopped?` for the shell's head board: an endpoint that never\n   bound is done on the first tick, because no host exists to report a\n   `:stopped` fact for it, an absent endpoint -- a shell run without\n   `--port` -- never had one, and so is a shell with no board.  The custody\n   composition is not stepped here: `shutdown-tick` is the drain tick that\n   wraps this one with the custody control plane.\"\n  [state server now]\n  (let [[repl dht-lines _] (repl.dht/step (:repl state) now)\n        state (assoc state :repl repl)\n        [server' entries] (if server\n                            (let [[entries server'] (serve/take-outbox\n                                                      (serve/step server now))]\n                              [server' entries])\n                            [nil []])]\n    [state server'\n     (into (vec dht-lines) (map entry-text) entries)\n     (and (or (nil? server') (serve/stopped? server'))\n          (repl.dht/stopped? repl))]))\n\n\n(defn shutdown-enter?\n  \"Whether a shell that just stopped enters the bounded shutdown drain at\n   all (r3 1.12 as amended by residual 4 and the D15 gate's shutdown row):\n   there is an endpoint to stop, a head board that still owes its\n   stop-and-drain, or a custody composition that still owes a\n   control-plane write.  A REPL run without --port enters the drain on its\n   owed custody writes alone -- a release owed at quit is sent, never\n   dropped by the process exit.\"\n  [state server]\n  (boolean (or server\n               (get-in state [:repl :dht])\n               (custody-owed? state))))\n\n\n(defn shutdown-drained?\n  \"The bounded shutdown drain's exit condition, the one every host loop\n   tests: the endpoint has stopped -- an absent endpoint owes nothing --\n   AND the shell's head board owes nothing at exit, AND the custody\n   composition owes no further control-plane write.  A pending release,\n   renewal or undelivered reply still owed keeps the drain stepping\n   within its `stop-ticks` budget; the budget, not this predicate, is\n   what bounds a write that can never complete.\"\n  [state server]\n  (boolean (and (serve/stopped? server)\n                (repl.dht/stopped? (:repl state))\n                (not (custody-owed? state)))))\n\n\n(defn shutdown-tick\n  \"One tick of the bounded shutdown drain (r3 1.12, residual 4), the tick\n   every host's drain loop calls: the custody control plane keeps running --\n   the holders' local stop latch is set here, at drain entry, on every host,\n   including paths that reached the drain without another normal `step-all`\n   -- and the head board and the endpoint drain (an absent endpoint drains\n   nothing and answers stopped).  No custody program step runs: program\n   execution stays stopped once the shell has.  Returns\n   `[state server lines stopped?]`; the budget (`stop-ticks`) and the exit\n   condition (`shutdown-enter?`, `shutdown-drained?`) are the caller's.\"\n  [state server now]\n  (let [state (custody-control-step (custody-stop state))]\n    (stop-tick state server now)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; clj: a reader thread that only appends, and one owned poller thread\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- drain-owner!\n  \"The shell has quit, so the head board, the endpoint and the custody\n        control plane stop before the host exits: ask the board and the\n        endpoint once, then keep stepping all three until the endpoint has\n        stopped AND the board owes nothing AND custody owes no further\n        control write (`shutdown-drained?`), or the bounded budget runs\n        out.  The drain is entered at all only when one of them owes\n        something (`shutdown-enter?` -- a shell with no endpoint enters it\n        on owed custody writes alone).  A connected client must observe the\n        ended answer, not the bare close a process exit would leave behind.\n        Each drain round lasts at least the base interval of wall time,\n        however many early wakes readiness causes; the budget counts rounds,\n        so the drain is at least `tick-millis x stop-ticks` of wall time.\n        The custody control plane steps on every one of its ticks\n        (`shutdown-tick`); the state it reaches is durable in the progress\n        journal, so what the budget leaves outstanding is a restart's to\n        resend.  Answers the drained state.\"\n  [state server now sleep!]\n  (if (shutdown-enter? state server)\n    (loop [state (update state :repl repl.dht/stop!)\n           server (some-> server serve/stop!)\n           remaining stop-ticks]\n      (let [[state' server' lines _stopped?]\n            (shutdown-tick state server (now))]\n        (doseq [line lines]\n          (println line))\n        (flush)\n        (cond\n          (shutdown-drained? state' server') state'\n          (zero? remaining) (do (println stop-timeout-text) state')\n          :else (let [round-end (+ (now) tick-millis)]\n                  ;; a sleep readiness ends early pumps, then sleeps the\n                  ;; rest: an early wake cannot spend a round (R17)\n                  (loop []\n                    (sleep! (max 0 (- round-end (now))))\n                    (when (< (now) round-end) (recur)))\n                  (recur state' server' (dec remaining))))))\n    state))\n\n\n(defn run-owner!\n  \"Serial shell owner shared by the JVM and jolt. Host policy supplies\n   clock, interruptible sleep and status-aware exit. No producer evaluates.\"\n  [state server headless? now sleep! exit!]\n  (loop [state state\n         server server\n         cadence (cadence/init default-cadence)]\n    (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server\n                                           (now))]\n      (doseq [line lines]\n        (println line))\n      (flush)\n      (if (:running? state')\n        (do (when (and (not headless?) (prompt-owed? state state' lines))\n              (do (print prompt) (flush)))\n            (let [{:keys [cadence-state sleep-ms]}\n                  (cadence/cadence-step cadence\n                                        (moved? state' server' lines))]\n              (sleep! sleep-ms)\n              (recur state' server' cadence-state)))\n        (let [state' (drain-owner! state' server' now sleep!)]\n          (println)\n          (close-index-store! state')\n          (flush)\n          (exit! (exit-status state')))))))\n\n\n#?(:cljd\n   nil\n   :jolt nil\n   :clj\n   (do\n     (defn- print-prompt!\n       []\n       (print prompt)\n       (flush))\n\n     (defn poll-loop!\n       \"JVM policy wrapper around the portable serial owner.\"\n       ([state server headless?]\n        (poll-loop! state server headless? #(.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)))\n       ([state server headless? exit!]\n        (poll-loop! state server headless? exit! (wake/make-wake)))\n       ([state server headless? exit! w]\n        (run-owner! state server headless? #(System/currentTimeMillis)\n                    #(wake/sleep! w %)\n                    (fn [status]\n                      (if (zero? status) (exit!)\n                          (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) (int status)))))))\n\n     (defn- read-loop!\n       \"The reader parks in `read-line` and appends, nudging the step owner's\n        wake: a typed line ends the idle sleep at once.  It reads no state.\"\n       [input w]\n       (loop []\n         (if-let [line (read-line)]\n           (do (driver/submit-line! input line)\n               (wake/nudge! w)\n               (recur))\n           (do (driver/submit-line! input \"(quit)\")\n               (wake/nudge! w)))))\n\n     (defn -main\n       [& args]\n       (let\n         [started (startup args {:persist? true})]\n         (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n         (if-some\n           [refusal (:refusal started)]\n           (refuse! refusal)\n           (let\n             [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n              headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))\n              w (wake/make-wake)]\n             (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n               (println line))\n             (let\n               [poller (Thread.\n                         ^Runnable (fn []\n                                     (poll-loop!\n                                       state\n                                       server\n                                       headless?\n                                       #(.halt\n                                          (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)\n                                       w)))]\n               (.setDaemon poller true)\n               (.start poller)\n               ;; Headless attends the endpoint only: there is no reader, so the\n               ;; step owner is joined until it stops, and the explicit stop\n               ;; trigger\n               ;; is the host signal a shutdown hook observes.  The hook appends\n               ;; a\n               ;; line like any producer, nudges, and then waits for the one\n               ;; step\n               ;; owner.\n               (if headless?\n                 (do (.addShutdownHook\n                       (Runtime/getRuntime)\n                       (Thread. ^Runnable (fn []\n                                            (request-stop! state w)\n                                            (.join poller ^long\n                                                   stop-join-millis))))\n                     (.join poller))\n                 (do (print-prompt!)\n                     ;; End-of-input is one way to stop; a typed `(quit)` is the\n                     ;; other, and the step owner has already exited the process\n                     ;; by\n                     ;; the time this join is reached in that case.\n                     ;; The join is unbounded: lines read before end-of-input\n                     ;; still evaluate ahead of its `(quit)`, and the step\n                     ;; owner bounds its own shutdown drain (`stop-ticks`)\n                     ;; and exits the process itself.\n                     (read-loop! (:input state) w)\n                     (.join poller))))\n             (.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)))))))\n\n\n#?(:jolt\n   (defn -main\n     \"One plain-thread owner. Stdin deposits only; EOF deposits (quit).\n      One host WebSocket instance serves the shell, the head board and\n      --port; every owner round polls its sockets (headless included) and\n      the instance is closed before exit, however the drain ended.\n      Saved flags persist in the node directory, as on the JVM (J4d):\n      --reset forgets them and --no-state neither reads nor writes them.\"\n     [& args]\n     (let [adapter (host/websocket)\n           started (startup args {:persist? true :adapter adapter})]\n       (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n       (if-some [refusal (:refusal started)]\n         (refuse! refusal)\n         (let [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n               headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))\n               eof? (atom false)\n               w (wake/make-wake)\n               line! (fn [line]\n                       (if (nil? line)\n                         (do (reset! eof? true)\n                             (driver/submit-line! (:input state) \"(quit)\"))\n                         (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)))\n               sleep! (fn [ms]\n                        (wake/sleep-fds! w ms (:pump adapter)\n                                         (when-not (or headless? @eof?) line!)))]\n           (doseq [line (banner opts)] (println line))\n           (when-not headless? (print prompt))\n           (flush)\n           ;; the exit status leaves through System/exit, which skips the\n           ;; finally, so the exit path closes the instance itself too\n           (try\n             (run-owner! state server headless? #(System/currentTimeMillis)\n                         sleep! (fn [status]\n                                  ((:close-host! adapter))\n                                  (wake/close! w)\n                                  (flush)\n                                  (System/exit status)))\n             (finally\n               ((:close-host! adapter))\n               (wake/close! w))))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; cljs (Node): a readline handler that only appends, and one interval owner\n;; =============================================================================\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (do\n     (defn run-node!\n       \"The tick owner on Node: one wake source arms exactly one timer per\n        round, at the interval `cadence-step` computes over\n        `default-cadence`.  Returns the wake so the composition can wire its\n        line producers (the readline handlers, the stop signals) as\n        `nudge!` callers.\n\n        `repl-step` is synchronous and the Node event loop is single\n        threaded, so a tick cannot overlap itself.  The box is host cadence\n        plumbing: the tick is the only reader and writer of it.  `:stopping`\n        is nil while the shell runs and a tick budget afterwards: the drain\n        is entered when there is an endpoint to stop, a head board that\n        still owes its stop-and-drain, or custody owes a control write\n        (`shutdown-enter?` -- a REPL without --port drains its owed custody\n        writes too), the head board and the endpoint are asked to stop\n        once, and the drain steps at the base interval until the endpoint\n        has stopped AND the board owes nothing AND custody owes nothing\n        (`shutdown-drained?`) or the budget ends.  `exit!`, the process\n        exit by default, is the seam a test injects to drive the owner to\n        its end.\"\n       ([state server rl] (run-node! state server rl #(js/process.exit %)))\n       ([state server rl exit!]\n        (let [box (atom {:state state :server server :stopping nil\n                         :cadence (cadence/init default-cadence)})\n              wake-ref (volatile! nil)\n              finish! (fn []\n                        (wake/disarm! @wake-ref)\n                        (when rl (.close rl))\n                        (close-index-store! (:state @box))\n                        (exit! (exit-status (:state @box))))\n              tick (fn []\n                     ;; The interval timer this namespace replaced fired\n                     ;; again whatever happened, so a tick whose body throws\n                     ;; must not kill the owner: arm the fallback first and\n                     ;; let the body's own `arm!` replace it. `finish!`\n                     ;; disarms, so a finished owner parks nothing.\n                     (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n                     (let [{:keys [state server stopping cadence]} @box]\n                       (if (nil? stopping)\n                         (let [[state' server' lines] (step-all state server\n                                                                (js/Date.now))]\n                           (reset! box {:state state'\n                                        :server server'\n                                        :stopping nil\n                                        :cadence cadence})\n                           (doseq [line lines]\n                             (js/console.log line))\n                           (cond\n                             (:running? state')\n                             (let [{:keys [cadence-state sleep-ms]}\n                                   (cadence/cadence-step\n                                     cadence (moved? state' server' lines))]\n                               (swap! box assoc :cadence cadence-state)\n                               (when (and rl (prompt-owed? state state' lines))\n                                 (.prompt rl))\n                               (wake/arm! @wake-ref sleep-ms))\n\n                             (shutdown-enter? state' server')\n                             (do (swap! box assoc\n                                        :state (update state' :repl repl.dht/stop!)\n                                        :server (when server'\n                                                  (serve/stop! server'))\n                                        :stopping stop-ticks)\n                                 (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis))\n\n                             :else (finish!)))\n                         (let [[state' server' lines _stopped?]\n                               (shutdown-tick state server (js/Date.now))]\n                           (swap! box assoc :state state' :server server')\n                           (doseq [line lines]\n                             (js/console.log line))\n                           (cond\n                             (shutdown-drained? state' server') (finish!)\n                             (zero? stopping)\n                             (do (js/console.log stop-timeout-text) (finish!))\n                             :else (do (swap! box assoc :stopping (dec stopping))\n                                       (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)))))))]\n          (vreset! wake-ref (wake/make-wake tick))\n          (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n          @wake-ref)))\n\n     (defn -main\n       [& args]\n       (let [started (startup args {:persist? true})]\n         (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n         (if-some [refusal (:refusal started)]\n           (refuse! refusal)\n           (let [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n                 readline (when-not (:headless? opts) (js/require \"readline\"))\n                 rl (when readline\n                      (.createInterface readline\n                                        #js {:input (.-stdin js/process)\n                                             :output (.-stdout js/process)\n                                             :prompt prompt}))]\n             (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n               (js/console.log line))\n             ;; The tick owner arms its first round, and the composition wires\n             ;; the\n             ;; deposit it hands each line producer with the nudge: a typed line\n             ;; ends the idle sleep at once.\n             (let [w (run-node! state server rl)]\n               (if rl\n                 (do (.on rl \"line\" (fn [line]\n                                      (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)\n                                      (wake/nudge! w)))\n                     (.on rl \"close\" (fn []\n                                       (driver/submit-line! (:input state)\n                                                            \"(quit)\")\n                                       (wake/nudge! w)))\n                     (.prompt rl))\n                 ;; Headless has no reader, so the explicit stop trigger is the\n                 ;; host\n                 ;; signal: it appends a line, nudges, and returns, like any\n                 ;; producer.\n                 (doseq [signal [\"SIGINT\" \"SIGTERM\"]]\n                   (.on js/process signal (fn []\n                                            (request-stop! state\n                                                           w))))))))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; cljd: a stdin listener that only appends, and one periodic timer owner\n;; =============================================================================\n\n#?(:cljd\n   (do\n     (defn- write-line!\n       [message]\n       (println message))\n\n     (defn- print-prompt!\n       []\n       (.write io/stdout prompt)\n       (.flush io/stdout))\n\n     (defn run-dart!\n       \"One wake source owns the tick: exactly one `Timer` armed per round, at\n        the interval `cadence-step` computes over `default-cadence`, because a\n        synchronous poll loop would deadlock the Dart event loop: IO never\n        progresses, so `blocked` never clears.  Returns the wake so the\n        composition can wire its line producers (the stdin listener, the\n        stop signals) as `nudge!` callers.\n\n        `:stopping` is nil while the shell runs and a tick budget afterwards:\n        the drain is entered when there is an endpoint to stop, a head\n        board that still owes its stop-and-drain, or custody owes a\n        control write (`shutdown-enter?` -- a REPL without --port drains\n        its owed custody writes too), the head board and the endpoint are\n        asked to stop once, and the drain steps at the base interval\n        until the endpoint has stopped AND the board owes nothing AND\n        custody owes nothing (`shutdown-drained?`) or the budget ends, so\n        the host does not exit with a live listener or a dropped\n        release.  `exit!`, the process exit by default, is the seam\n        a test injects to drive the owner to its end.\"\n       ([state server headless?]\n        (run-dart! state server headless? #(io/exit %)))\n       ([state server headless? exit!]\n        (let [box (atom {:state state :server server :stopping nil\n                         :cadence (cadence/init default-cadence)})\n              wake-ref (volatile! nil)\n              finish! (fn []\n                        (wake/disarm! @wake-ref)\n                        (close-index-store! (:state @box))\n                        (exit! (exit-status (:state @box)))\n                        nil)\n              tick (fn []\n                     ;; The periodic timer this namespace replaced fired\n                     ;; again whatever happened, so a tick whose body throws\n                     ;; must not kill the owner: arm the fallback first and\n                     ;; let the body's own `arm!` replace it. `finish!`\n                     ;; disarms, so a finished owner parks nothing.\n                     (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n                     (let [{:keys [state server stopping cadence]} @box\n                           now (.-millisecondsSinceEpoch\n                                 (dart-core/DateTime.now))]\n                       (if (nil? stopping)\n                         (let [[state' server' lines]\n                               (step-all state server now)]\n                           (reset! box {:state state'\n                                        :server server'\n                                        :stopping nil\n                                        :cadence cadence})\n                           (doseq [line lines]\n                             (write-line! line))\n                           (cond\n                             (:running? state')\n                             (let [{:keys [cadence-state sleep-ms]}\n                                   (cadence/cadence-step\n                                     cadence (moved? state' server' lines))]\n                               (swap! box assoc :cadence cadence-state)\n                               (when (and (not headless?)\n                                          (prompt-owed? state state' lines))\n                                 (print-prompt!))\n                               (wake/arm! @wake-ref sleep-ms))\n\n                             (shutdown-enter? state' server')\n                             (do (swap! box assoc\n                                        :state (update state' :repl repl.dht/stop!)\n                                        :server (when server'\n                                                  (serve/stop! server'))\n                                        :stopping stop-ticks)\n                                 (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n                                 nil)\n\n                             :else (finish!)))\n                         (let [[state' server' lines _stopped?]\n                               (shutdown-tick state server now)]\n                           (swap! box assoc :state state' :server server')\n                           (doseq [line lines]\n                             (write-line! line))\n                           (cond\n                             (shutdown-drained? state' server') (finish!)\n                             (zero? stopping) (do (write-line! stop-timeout-text)\n                                                  (finish!))\n                             :else (do (swap! box assoc :stopping (dec stopping))\n                                       (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n                                       nil))))))]\n          (vreset! wake-ref (wake/make-wake tick))\n          (wake/arm! @wake-ref tick-millis)\n          @wake-ref)))\n\n     (defn -main\n       [& args]\n       (let [started (startup args {:persist? true})]\n         (when (contains? started :exit) (exit-with! started))\n         (if-some [refusal (:refusal started)]\n           (refuse! refusal)\n           (let [{:keys [opts state server]} started\n                 headless? (boolean (:headless? opts))]\n             (doseq [line (banner opts)]\n               (write-line! line))\n             ;; The tick owner arms its first round, and the composition wires\n             ;; the\n             ;; deposit it hands each line producer with the nudge: a typed line\n             ;; ends the idle sleep at once.  A timer fires only on the event\n             ;; loop, so arming before the producers are wired races nothing.\n             (let [w (run-dart! state server headless?)]\n               (if headless?\n                 ;; Headless has no reader, so the explicit stop trigger is the\n                 ;; host signal: it appends a line, nudges, and returns, like\n                 ;; any\n                 ;; producer.\n                 (-> (.watch io/ProcessSignal.sigint)\n                     (.listen (fn [_signal] (request-stop! state w))))\n                 (do (-> io/stdin\n                         (.transform (.-decoder convert/utf8))\n                         (.transform (convert/LineSplitter.))\n                         (.listen (fn [line]\n                                    (driver/submit-line! (:input state) line)\n                                    (wake/nudge! w))\n                                  .onDone (fn []\n                                            (driver/submit-line! (:input state)\n                                                                 \"(quit)\")\n                                            (wake/nudge! w))))\n                     (print-prompt!))))))))\n\n     (defn ^{:dart/name main} run-main\n       [args]\n       (apply -main args))))\n(ns yin.repl.index\n  \"The shell's code indexer: a `dao.space.index` observer on `program-out`\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md).\n\n   It is a peer of the evaluator's observer on the same medium, attached\n   independently and advanced by the evaluation round, never by the\n   evaluator: neither knows the other.  Each expanded tree packet `[root\n   rows]` it observes is projected to local `[e a v t m]` AST facts\n   (yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md §6.5) and committed as ONE atomic transaction\n   record through a `dao.space.transactor`, which allocates `t`; this\n   namespace supplies no clock.  Every row of a committed packet is\n   materialized into the `dao.jing` store in the same round, so its\n   `:yin/address` facts name content the store holds (yin.vm.linker.dht.md\n   5.1, rows every round).  After a round that committed, the covered\n   indexes are published through `dao.space.index` into that round's\n   own fresh intake, the intake is drained into the `dao.jing` store, the\n   manifest is read back, and the intake is dropped. Between rounds the\n   composition retains the transactor's allocator and a verified covered\n   frontier: its log cursor, manifest and transaction count. Each fresh\n   publication folds only the committed suffix into the previous covered\n   roots and emits their dirty subgraph through `dao.space.index`.\n\n   Publication still completes synchronously every evaluation. Initial\n   publication and recovery reconstruct the full authoritative history;\n   no saved process cursor or allocator is trusted on restart. Incremental\n   tree mutation removes repeated prefix construction, but does not promise\n   a fixed wall-time bound on hashing, storage or other round work.\n\n   Every `e` is a transactor-local entity id; the only durable name in the\n   projection is the `:yin/address` fact each node occurrence carries back\n   to its row address.  `m` is a local metadata entity, one per program,\n   carrying `:db/op`, the shell's stable session token, the program's root\n   address, and the round.  Only code is indexed: no printed output, last\n   value, or evaluation result enters.\n\n   Loss is reported, never hidden, and never stops the evaluator (owner\n   ruling, 2026-09-28): a gap is counted on the observer (`:ingress-gaps`)\n   and marks the indexer `:lost?`, after which it consumes packets without\n   indexing any until reset rebuilds it; a packet that could not be\n   committed, or a publication that did not become readable, is recorded\n   under `:failure`, never counted as indexed.  `notice` renders what a\n   round lost for the shell's round result, and `status` is the\n   evaluator-independent view `repl-state` reports.  Everything here is a\n   session-composition value; reset and VM selection rebuild it with the\n   session.  Over a durable store a new process's indexer is seeded from\n   the recovered snapshot (`rehydrate`), and a rebuilt session's continues\n   the old one's index (`carry-over`), so `t` and entity allocation never\n   restart there.\"\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.space.index :as index]\n            [dao.space.transactor :as transactor]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.memory-log :as memory-log]\n            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.macro :as macro]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The §6.5 projection\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- assign-local-ids\n  \"Give every node occurrence of `node`, the map AST rebuilt from row `id`,\n   a fresh local entity id as its `:eid`, pairing it with its row through\n   the §2.3 slot table.  Distinct occurrences of one shared row get distinct\n   ids, so `vm/ast->datoms` emits every occurrence exactly as it does for a\n   tree without `:eid`s.  `acc` is `[next-e addresses]`; answers\n   `[node' acc']`, `addresses` mapping each id to its row address.\"\n  [rows node id [next-e addresses]]\n  (let [row (get rows id)\n        slots (get vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar (:type node))\n        child (fn [acc child-node child-id]\n                (assign-local-ids rows child-node child-id acc))]\n    (reduce\n      (fn [[node acc] [i [field kind]]]\n        (let [slot (nth row (+ 2 i))]\n          (case kind\n            :node (let [[c acc'] (child acc (get node field) slot)]\n                    [(assoc node field c) acc'])\n            :nodes (let [[cs acc']\n                         (reduce (fn [[cs acc] [c cid]]\n                                   (let [[c' acc'] (child acc c cid)]\n                                     [(conj cs c') acc']))\n                                 [[] acc]\n                                 (map vector (get node field) slot))]\n                     [(assoc node field cs) acc'])\n            [node acc])))\n      [(assoc node :eid next-e) [(inc next-e) (assoc addresses next-e id)]]\n      (map-indexed vector slots))))\n\n\n(defn packet->tx-data\n  \"Project one expanded tree packet to transactor tx-data, allocating local\n   entity ids from `next-e`.  `provenance` is `{:session-token s :round r}`.\n\n   Answers `{:tx-data [...] :next-e n :meta-e m :root root}`: the metadata\n   entity's facts, then `vm/ast->datoms` of the expanded tree under `m`,\n   then one `[e :yin/address addr]` per node occurrence.  Every datom leaves\n   `t` nil, which the transactor stamps.\"\n  [packet next-e {:keys [session-token round]}]\n  (let [{:keys [root rows], :as row-set} (macro/packet->row-set packet)\n        meta-e next-e\n        [ast [next-e' addresses]] (assign-local-ids\n                                    rows\n                                    (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast row-set)\n                                    root\n                                    [(inc meta-e) {}])\n        facts (vm/ast->datoms ast {:m meta-e})\n        untimed (fn [e a v m] [e a v nil m])]\n    (when-let [stray (some (fn [[e]] (when (neg? e) e)) facts)]\n      (throw (ex-info \"projected node escaped local id assignment\"\n                      {:e stray, :root root})))\n    {:tx-data (-> [(untimed meta-e :db/op :db/assert datom/default-op)\n                   (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/session session-token\n                            datom/default-op)\n                   (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/root root datom/default-op)\n                   (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/round round datom/default-op)]\n                  (into (map (fn [[e a v _t m]] (untimed e a v m))) facts)\n                  (into (map (fn [[e addr]]\n                               (untimed e :yin/address addr meta-e)))\n                        (sort-by key addresses))),\n     :next-e next-e',\n     :meta-e meta-e,\n     :root root\n     :row-set row-set}))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The indexer value\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- memory-log\n  []\n  (:dao.stream/handle\n    (memory-log/create! {:dao.stream/type memory-log/transport-type})))\n\n\n(defn make-indexer\n  \"The indexer for one session: `observer`, attached to `program-out`\n   beside the evaluator's; `:local`, the complete-retention log its\n   transactions are committed to (dao.space.transactor requires memory-log\n   retention); and `content-store`, the `dao.jing` byte store publications\n   materialize into.  `session-token` is the shell's stable token, recorded\n   as provenance; `publish-opts` reach `transactor/publish!` as-is.\n\n   `after-publish` is `(fn [manifest-address])`, called after a round's\n   manifest is read back from the store and before the round is reported\n   published: the durable store's HEAD write, which is what makes the\n   round durably published (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md).\n   Nil — the memory store — publishes exactly as before.\n\n   `:allocator` retains only the transactor authority, with its intake\n   detached. `:materialization` retains verified coverage as plain data:\n   `{:cursor c :transactions n :manifest-address a :manifest m :max-t t}`.\n   A round's fresh `:publication` intake, pool and tree builder are dropped\n   at its end; no earlier publication payload is retained.\"\n  [{:keys [observer session-token content-store publish-opts after-publish]}]\n  {:observer observer\n   :local (memory-log)\n   :content-store content-store\n   :session-token session-token\n   :publish-opts publish-opts\n   :after-publish after-publish\n   :next-e datom/first-user-id\n   :transactions 0\n   :published 0\n   :published-payloads 0\n   :manifest-address nil\n   :allocator nil\n   :materialization nil\n   :lost? false\n   :failure nil})\n\n\n(defn- next-entity-id\n  \"One greater than the greatest entity or metadata-entity id `datoms`\n   use, never below `datom/first-user-id`.\"\n  [datoms]\n  (reduce (fn [next-e [e _a _v _t m]]\n            (cond-> next-e\n              (integer? e) (max (inc e))\n              (integer? m) (max (inc m))))\n          datom/first-user-id\n          datoms))\n\n\n(defn rehydrate\n  \"Seed a fresh indexer from a recovered durable snapshot, `{:manifest\n   address :datoms [...]}` (yin.repl.store's `:recovery`), before any\n   evaluation is admitted.  Its log becomes a fresh complete-retention\n   memory log holding the recovered datoms as transaction records, one\n   per original `t` in ascending order, each datom and its `t` preserved,\n   so a new transactor derives the next `t` from it. Entity\n   allocation resumes one past the greatest restored entity or metadata\n   id.  The manifest is the published one, covering every restored\n   transaction, so `q` answers from it before anything new is evaluated.\n   Restored facts keep their original session tokens and round metadata:\n   they are the datoms as committed.  A recovery without a manifest is an\n   empty index. Live allocation and publication successors are always\n   discarded, including any stale or foreign cursor: the first subsequent\n   publication takes the full reconstruction path and derives a new cursor\n   from this log, never from a saved process checkpoint.\"\n  [indexer {:keys [manifest datoms]}]\n  (if (nil? manifest)\n    (assoc indexer :allocator nil :materialization nil)\n    (let [log (memory-log)\n          by-t (group-by index/datom-t datoms)\n          ts (sort (keys by-t))]\n      (doseq [t ts]\n        (let [answer (stream/append! log {:dao.space/transaction\n                                          {:t t, :datoms (vec (get by-t t))}})]\n          (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answer))\n            (throw (ex-info \"the recovered snapshot could not be rehydrated\"\n                            {:t t, :outcome answer})))))\n      (assoc indexer\n             :local log\n             :next-e (next-entity-id datoms)\n             :transactions (count ts)\n             :published (count ts)\n             :allocator nil\n             :materialization nil\n             :manifest-address manifest))))\n\n\n(defn carry-over\n  \"A rebuilt session's fresh `indexer`, continuing `previous`'s index: its\n   transaction log, entity allocation, counts, published manifest, and\n   live allocator/verified materialization successors.\n   `(reset)` and VM selection over a durable store use it, so a rebuild\n   never resets `t` or entity allocation and `q` keeps answering the\n   published index; the observer is the new session's, and a lost or\n   failed indexer is recovered by the rebuild as it is today.\"\n  [indexer previous]\n  (merge indexer\n         (select-keys previous [:local :next-e :transactions :published\n                                :published-payloads :manifest-address\n                                :allocator :materialization :unwritten])))\n\n\n(defn- open-publication\n  \"The round's transactor and the `dao.jing` pool observer over its intake.\n   The retained allocator remains the transactor's authority; when absent\n   or bound to another log, create! derives it from authoritative history.\n   No caller-supplied clock is accepted. The intake is\n   complete-retention: `publish-index!` appends every node blob before the\n   manifest and the round drains only after it returns, so an evicting\n   intake would lose the head of any publication larger than its capacity\n   and that publication could never complete.\"\n  [ix]\n  (if (:publication ix)\n    ix\n    (let [intake (memory-log)\n          allocator (:allocator ix)\n          tx (if (and allocator\n                      (identical? (:local ix) (:local-stream allocator)))\n               (transactor/with-intake-pool allocator [intake])\n               (transactor/create! {:local-stream (:local ix)\n                                    :intake-pool [intake]\n                                    :name \"yin.repl.index\"}))]\n      (assoc ix\n             :allocator (transactor/with-intake-pool tx nil)\n             :publication\n             {:transactor tx\n              :intake intake\n              :pool (jing/observer-state\n                      [{:stream intake\n                        :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n                                  (stream/cursor intake\n                                                 :dao.stream/oldest))}])}))))\n\n\n(defn- write-rows\n  \"Put the rows of every committed program not yet written into the index\n   store, oldest first (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 5.1, owner decision 1: rows\n   every round), so `:yin/address` facts name content the store holds.\n   `:unwritten` holds those programs' row sets.  A put that fails stops\n   the writing: it and every later row set stay `:unwritten`, and the\n   round's failure is stage `:materialize`.  Once nothing is unwritten, a\n   `:materialize` failure is recovered and cleared.  No publication is\n   made while anything is unwritten, so a HEAD never moves over a missing\n   row.\"\n  [ix round]\n  (loop [ix ix]\n    (if-let [row-set (first (:unwritten ix))]\n      (let [failed (try (vm/materialize-tree! (:content-store ix) row-set)\n                        nil\n                        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n                          {:round round\n                           :stage :materialize\n                           :root (:root row-set)\n                           :message (ex-message e)}))]\n        (if failed\n          (assoc ix :failure failed)\n          (recur (update ix :unwritten (comp vec rest)))))\n      (cond-> (dissoc ix :unwritten)\n        (= :materialize (:stage (:failure ix))) (dissoc :failure)))))\n\n\n(defn- commit\n  \"Commit one packet as one transaction; its rows join `:unwritten`, which\n   the round writes before it publishes (`write-rows`).  A packet that\n   could not be projected or committed is consumed and recorded as the\n   failure, never counted, and materializes nothing.\"\n  [ix packet round]\n  (try\n    (let [{:keys [tx-data next-e root row-set]}\n          (packet->tx-data packet\n                           (:next-e ix)\n                           {:session-token (:session-token ix), :round round})\n          answer (transactor/transact! (get-in ix [:publication :transactor])\n                                       tx-data)]\n      (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answer))\n        [(-> ix\n             (assoc :next-e next-e)\n             (update :transactions inc)\n             (update :unwritten (fnil conj []) row-set))\n         true]\n        [(assoc ix\n                :failure {:round round\n                          :root root\n                          :stage :transact\n                          :outcome (:dao.stream/outcome answer)})\n         false]))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n      [(assoc ix\n              :failure {:round round\n                        :stage :project\n                        :message (ex-message e)})\n       false])))\n\n\n(defn- materialize\n  \"Drain the intake into the store until it blocks.  Answers how many\n   payloads it materialized and, when the drain met a gap or a defect, what\n   it met.  The pool is not kept: its intake is dropped with the round.\"\n  [content-store pool]\n  (loop [pool pool\n         n 0]\n    (let [{:keys [signal], :as r} (jing/observe-step! content-store pool)]\n      (case signal\n        :dao.stream/ok\n        (let [missing #?(:cljd (Object.) :clj (Object.) :cljs (js-obj))]\n          ;; Every emitted dirty node reads back before HEAD moves. The\n          ;; unchanged subgraphs were verified by earlier publications;\n          ;; immutable retained content makes that verification reusable.\n          (when (identical? missing (jing/get content-store (:address r) missing))\n            (throw (ex-info \"published content could not be read back\"\n                            {:address (:address r)})))\n          (recur (:state r) (inc n)))\n        :dao.stream/blocked [n nil]\n        [n signal]))))\n\n\n(defn- suffix-state\n  \"Restore verified roots and fold only records after their cursor. The\n   expected record count independently validates the cursor against this\n   composition's one-record-per-transaction log. A stale/foreign cursor,\n   mismatched frontier or rejected record returns nil: the caller must\n   reconstruct in full, never publish an uncertain suffix.\"\n  [ix intake]\n  (let [{:keys [cursor transactions manifest-address manifest max-t]}\n        (:materialization ix)]\n    (when (and manifest-address\n               (= manifest-address (:manifest-address ix))\n               (= transactions (:published ix))\n               (= (:branching-factor manifest)\n                  (:branching-factor (:publish-opts ix) 512)))\n      (try\n        (let [candidate {:manifest-address manifest-address\n                         :mode :resolved :schema-address (jing/segment-key {})\n                         :ids nil :batch transactions :max-t max-t :rejected 0}\n              consumer (index/restore candidate\n                                      {:content-store (:content-store ix)\n                                       :intake intake :mode :resolved :schema {}})\n              expected (- (:transactions ix) transactions)]\n          (loop [cursor cursor, consumer consumer, n 0]\n            (let [r (stream/next (:local ix) cursor)]\n              (when (stream/valid-outcome? :next r)\n                (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n                  :dao.stream/ok\n                  (when (< n expected)\n                    (let [consumer' (index/fold-batch consumer (:dao.stream/value r))]\n                      (when (zero? (:rejected consumer'))\n                        (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) consumer' (inc n)))))\n                  (:dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end)\n                  (when (= n expected) {:cursor cursor :consumer consumer})\n                  nil)))))\n        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) _\n          nil)))))\n\n\n(defn- enqueue-publication\n  \"Full reconstruction when no validated frontier is available; otherwise\n   publication of the suffix-folded trees through the existing indexer.\"\n  [ix]\n  (let [{:keys [transactor intake]} (:publication ix)]\n    (if-let [{:keys [cursor consumer]} (suffix-state ix intake)]\n      (let [;; A fresh round has exactly one intake; still honor the\n            ;; caller's selection function and its pool-membership rule.\n            select-stream (:select-stream (:publish-opts ix) first)\n            selected (when (fn? select-stream) (select-stream [intake]))\n            _ (when-not (identical? selected intake)\n                (throw (ex-info \"publication must select its round's intake\" {})))\n            {:keys [status state]} (index/publish! consumer)\n            manifest (get-in state [:publish :manifest])]\n        (when-not (= :ok status)\n          (throw (ex-info \"complete-retention publication intake refused\" {:status status})))\n        {:cursor cursor :manifest manifest :manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest)})\n      (let [result (transactor/publish! transactor (:publish-opts ix))\n            tail (stream/cursor (:local ix) :dao.stream/newest)]\n        (when-not (and (stream/valid-outcome? :cursor tail)\n                       (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome tail)))\n          (throw (ex-info \"published log frontier could not be read\" {:result tail})))\n        (assoc result :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor tail))))))\n\n\n(defn- publish\n  \"Publish the covered indexes over every committed transaction, using\n   the retained frontier when valid, drain the intake, and read the\n   manifest back from it.  Only a publication whose manifest the store\n   answers becomes the reported one, covering `:published` transactions;\n   in durable mode the HEAD pointer moves — `after-publish` — only after\n   that read-back, so a HEAD never names a manifest the store cannot\n   answer, and a HEAD that cannot be written leaves the round reported\n   unpublished.\"\n  [ix round]\n  (try\n    (let [{:keys [pool]} (:publication ix)\n          {:keys [manifest-address manifest cursor]} (enqueue-publication ix)\n          [n defect] (materialize (:content-store ix) pool)]\n      (if defect\n        (assoc ix :failure {:round round :stage :materialize :outcome defect})\n        (do (index/read-manifest (:content-store ix) manifest-address)\n            (when-some [write-head! (:after-publish ix)]\n              (write-head! manifest-address))\n            (cond-> (assoc ix\n                           :manifest-address manifest-address\n                           :published (:transactions ix)\n                           :published-payloads n\n                           :materialization {:cursor cursor\n                                             :transactions (:transactions ix)\n                                             :manifest-address manifest-address\n                                             :manifest manifest\n                                             :max-t (dec (transactor/watermark (:allocator ix)))})\n              (= :publish (:stage (:failure ix))) (assoc :failure nil)))))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n      (assoc ix\n             :failure {:round round :stage :publish :message (ex-message e)}))))\n\n\n(defn- consume\n  \"Advance the observer past every packet still on the medium, handing each\n   to `f` with the indexer; a gap marks the indexer lost, after which no\n   packet reaches `f`.\"\n  [indexer f]\n  (loop [ix indexer]\n    (let [{:keys [status batch] observer' :observer}\n          (observer/observe-next (:observer ix))\n          ix (assoc ix :observer observer')]\n      (case status\n        :ok (recur (if (:lost? ix) ix (f ix batch)))\n        :gap (recur (assoc ix :lost? true))\n        ix))))\n\n\n(defn step\n  \"The round's index step: observe every packet `program-out` still holds\n   for this observer, commit each through the round's publication, publish\n   when any was committed, then drop the publication's intake, pool and\n   temporary trees. Only the detached allocator and verified covered\n   frontier continue into the next round. A gap is counted on the observer's\n   `:ingress-gaps`, and marks the indexer lost: the missing packet and\n   every later one are never called indexed.\"\n  [indexer round]\n  (let [ix (consume indexer\n                    (fn [ix packet]\n                      (first (commit (open-publication ix) packet round))))\n        committed? (> (:transactions ix) (:transactions indexer))\n        ;; this round's rows and any an earlier round could not write,\n        ;; before any publication: no announcement names a missing row\n        ix (if committed? (write-rows ix round) ix)]\n    (dissoc (if (and committed? (empty? (:unwritten ix)))\n              (publish ix round)\n              ix)\n            :publication)))\n\n\n(defn commit-names\n  \"Commit the signed name envelopes `signed` -- each `{:envelope e :proof\n   p}` -- as ONE transaction, then publish the covered indexes at once,\n   the HEAD write included (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.1): one\n   entity per envelope carrying `:yin.module/envelope` and\n   `:yin.module/proof`, with the session's metadata entity -- `:db/op`,\n   the session token and `round`, no program root -- in `m`.  A\n   transaction refused is recorded as the failure, like a packet's.\"\n  [indexer signed round]\n  (let [ix (open-publication indexer)\n        meta-e (:next-e ix)\n        untimed (fn [e a v m] [e a v nil m])\n        tx-data (into [(untimed meta-e :db/op :db/assert datom/default-op)\n                       (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/session (:session-token ix)\n                                datom/default-op)\n                       (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/round round datom/default-op)]\n                      (comp (map-indexed\n                              (fn [i {:keys [envelope proof]}]\n                                (let [e (+ meta-e 1 i)]\n                                  [(untimed e :yin.module/envelope envelope meta-e)\n                                   (untimed e :yin.module/proof proof meta-e)])))\n                            cat)\n                      signed)\n        answer (transactor/transact! (get-in ix [:publication :transactor])\n                                     tx-data)]\n    (dissoc (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answer))\n              (let [ix (-> ix\n                           (assoc :next-e (+ meta-e 1 (count signed)))\n                           (update :transactions inc)\n                           (write-rows round))]\n                (cond-> ix (empty? (:unwritten ix)) (publish round)))\n              (assoc ix\n                     :failure {:round round\n                               :stage :transact\n                               :outcome (:dao.stream/outcome answer)}))\n            :publication)))\n\n\n(defn skip\n  \"Advance the observer past every packet still on the medium without\n   indexing it: a round that threw before its program ran executes nothing,\n   so nothing is indexed.  A gap still marks the indexer lost.\"\n  [indexer]\n  (consume indexer (fn [ix _packet] ix)))\n\n\n(defn status\n  \"The indexer's state as the shell reports it.  It depends on the programs\n   evaluated, never on which evaluator ran them or on the shell's token,\n   so every VM reports the same status for the same inputs.\"\n  [indexer]\n  {:transactions (:transactions indexer)\n   :published? (and (some? (:manifest-address indexer))\n                    (= (:published indexer) (:transactions indexer)))\n   :lost? (:lost? indexer)\n   :gaps (:ingress-gaps (:observer indexer) 0)\n   :failure (:failure indexer)})\n\n\n(defn notice\n  \"What the round from `before` to `after` must tell the user, or nil: a\n   lost indexer (the round's code was not indexed), or a new failure (the\n   round's code was not committed or its publication is not readable).\n   A failure's `:stage` is `:project`, `:transact`, `:materialize` or\n   `:publish`.\"\n  [before after]\n  (let [{:keys [stage outcome message]} (:failure after)]\n    (cond\n      (:lost? after)\n      (str \"Warning: the code index lost a program batch; this round's code\"\n           \" was not indexed, and indexing is suspended until (reset)\")\n\n      (and (= :materialize (:stage (:failure before))) (nil? (:failure after)))\n      (str \"Note: the code index caught up: rows of earlier rounds are now\"\n           \" written, and this round is published\")\n\n      (not= (:failure before) (:failure after))\n      (str \"Warning: this round's code is not in the published code index (\"\n           (name stage) \" failed\"\n           (when-let [detail (or message (some-> outcome name))]\n             (str \": \" detail))\n           \")\"))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj src/dev/jolt_workload_w_run.clj'","aggregated_output":"(ns jolt-workload-w\n  \"J6g Workload W harness and peer cross-host integration (BRIEF-j6g.md).\n   Pre-registered criterion (v3 Section 2 A1, amended by v3.1 D4):\n   One jolt process serving two peers on the same machine while the REPL evaluates\n   a submitted loop each round; peer A sends one binary message at the\n   composition's :ws/max-frame-bytes (4,194,304 bytes); peer B exchanges 1 KiB\n   request/reply messages concurrently, up to 8 outstanding, at 10 Hz, for the\n   whole of A's transfer and >= 60 s (>= 600 overlapping round trips; fewer than\n   500 samples is an invalid run).\n   Criterion: during A's transfer, B's round-trip p99 <= 250 ms, no reply lost;\n   A's message delivered once; the REPL step count advances every owner round.\n\n   Deliverable 1: The workload W harness and sampler.\n   Deliverable 2: Peer cross-host rows (hosts that dial/listen, reachability\n   refusals on the REPL path).\n   Deliverable 3: REPL served-stream rejoin peer probe.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [jolt.io-poller :as poller]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.stream.remote-channel :as remote-channel]\n            [dao.stream.rpc :as rpc]\n            [dao.stream.waitset.driver :as wake]\n            [dao.stream.ws :as ws]\n            [dao.stream.ws.jolt :as wsj]\n            [dao.stream.ws.rfc6455 :as rfc6455]\n            [dao.stream.ws.tcp :as tcp]\n            [dao.stream.ws.upgrade :as upgrade]\n            [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n            [jolt.socket.native :as native]\n            [yin.repl.connect :as connect]\n            [yin.repl.driver :as driver]\n            [yin.repl.host :as host]\n            [yin.repl.main :as repl.main]\n            [yin.repl.serve :as serve]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Raw TCP helpers (probe sockets outside the adapter)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- bvec\n  [bs]\n  (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 0xFF) (vec bs)))\n\n\n(defn- octets\n  [v]\n  (byte-array (map unchecked-byte v)))\n\n\n(defn- ascii\n  [v]\n  (apply str (map char v)))\n\n\n(defn- with-native\n  [n f]\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc n)]\n    (try (f p)\n         (finally (ffi/free p)))))\n\n\n(defn- raw-peer!\n  [port]\n  (let [{:keys [fd state]} (tcp/connect! (tcp/parse-address \"127.0.0.1\" port))]\n    (when (= :connecting state)\n      (native/poll-one fd native/pollout 2000)\n      (tcp/connect-result fd))\n    {:fd fd, :in (atom []), :end (atom nil)}))\n\n\n(defn- raw-poll!\n  [{:keys [fd in end]}]\n  (when-not @end\n    (with-native 65536\n      (fn [p]\n        (loop []\n          (let [r (tcp/recv-chunk! fd p 65536)]\n            (cond\n              (= :would-block r) nil\n              (= :eof r) (reset! end :eof)\n              (:error r) (reset! end (:class r))\n              :else (do (swap! in into (bvec (ffi/read-array p (:n r))))\n                        (recur)))))))))\n\n\n(defn- write-all!\n  ([fd bs]\n   (write-all! fd bs (+ (poller/clock-ms) 300000)))\n  ([fd bs deadline]\n   (let [bs (if (bytes/bytes? bs) bs (octets bs))\n         n (alength bs)\n         max-stalls 100]\n     (with-native (max 1 n)\n       (fn [p]\n         (loop [off 0\n                stalls 0]\n           (when (>= (poller/clock-ms) deadline)\n             (throw (ex-info \"raw write deadline exceeded\"\n                             {:workload/failure :timeout, :off off, :total n})))\n           (if (>= off n)\n             off\n             (do\n               ;; A short send must resume at the unsent byte, not byte zero.\n               (ffi/write-array p bs off (- n off))\n               (let [r (tcp/send-chunk! fd p (- n off))]\n                 (cond\n                   (:error r)\n                   (throw (ex-info \"raw write failed\" {:error r}))\n\n                   (and (map? r) (number? (:n r)) (pos? (:n r)) (<= (:n r) (- n off)))\n                   (recur (+ off (:n r)) 0)\n\n                   (or (= :would-block r) (= 0 (:n r)))\n                   (let [remaining (max 0 (long (- deadline (poller/clock-ms))))\n                         _ (native/poll-one fd native/pollout (min 2000 remaining))\n                         stalls (inc stalls)]\n                     (if (>= stalls max-stalls)\n                       (throw (ex-info \"TRANSFER STALLED: raw write made no progress\"\n                                       {:workload/failure :transfer-stalled,\n                                        :off off, :total n, :stalls stalls, :result r}))\n                       (recur off stalls)))\n\n                   :else\n                   (throw (ex-info \"unexpected raw write result\" {:result r}))))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- write-some!\n  [fd bs off limit]\n  (let [bs (if (bytes/bytes? bs) bs (octets bs))\n        n (min (- (alength bs) off) limit)]\n    (with-native n\n      (fn [p]\n        (ffi/write-array p bs off n)\n        (let [r (tcp/send-chunk! fd p n)]\n          (cond\n            (= :would-block r) :would-block\n            (:error r) (throw (ex-info \"raw write-some failed\" {:error r}))\n            (map? r) (:n r)\n            :else n))))))\n\n\n(defn- head-end\n  [v]\n  (let [n (count v)]\n    (loop [i 3]\n      (cond\n        (>= i n) nil\n        (and (= 13 (nth v (- i 3)))\n             (= 10 (nth v (- i 2)))\n             (= 13 (nth v (- i 1)))\n             (= 10 (nth v i)))\n        (inc i)\n        :else (recur (inc i))))))\n\n\n(defn- raw-close!\n  [peer]\n  (tcp/shutdown! (:fd peer) :both)\n  (tcp/close! (:fd peer)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Int32 Big-Endian packing for request IDs\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- int32->bytes!\n  [bs off val]\n  (bytes/set! bs off (bit-and (bit-shift-right val 24) 0xFF))\n  (bytes/set! bs (+ off 1) (bit-and (bit-shift-right val 16) 0xFF))\n  (bytes/set! bs (+ off 2) (bit-and (bit-shift-right val 8) 0xFF))\n  (bytes/set! bs (+ off 3) (bit-and val 0xFF))\n  bs)\n\n\n(defn- bytes->int32\n  [bs off]\n  (bit-or (bit-shift-left (bit-and (bytes/get bs off) 0xFF) 24)\n          (bit-shift-left (bit-and (bytes/get bs (+ off 1)) 0xFF) 16)\n          (bit-shift-left (bit-and (bytes/get bs (+ off 2)) 0xFF) 8)\n          (bit-and (bytes/get bs (+ off 3)) 0xFF)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Deliverable 1: Workload W Harness\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- make-1kib-request\n  [id]\n  (let [bs (bytes/alloc 1024)]\n    (int32->bytes! bs 0 id)\n    bs))\n\n\n(defn- check-deadline!\n  [deadline phase]\n  (when (>= (poller/clock-ms) deadline)\n    (throw (ex-info \"Workload W exceeded its five-minute deadline\"\n                    {:workload/failure :timeout, :phase phase}))))\n\n\n(defn run-workload-w\n  \"Run the pre-registered Workload W benchmark:\n   - Peer A: one 4,194,304 byte binary message (:ws/max-frame-bytes) paced over >= 60 s.\n   - Peer B: 1 KiB request/reply concurrent (up to 8 outstanding) at 10 Hz.\n   - REPL loop: evaluates a submitted loop each round, step count advances every round.\n   - Criterion: p99 <= 250 ms, no reply lost, A delivered once, REPL step advances every round.\n   Run through bb workload-w:jolt for the independent five-minute watchdog.\n   Returns {:pass? ... :p99 ... :samples ... :elapsed-ms ... :delivered-once? ... :repl-advances? ...}.\"\n  [& {:keys [target-duration-ms target-samples]\n      :or {target-duration-ms 60000 target-samples 600}}]\n  (println \"================================================================================\")\n  (println \"J6g Workload W: Starting benchmark (target duration:\" target-duration-ms\n           \"ms, target samples:\" target-samples \")\")\n  (println \"================================================================================\")\n  (let [run-start (poller/clock-ms)\n        deadline (+ run-start 300000)\n        w (wake/make-wake)\n        server-inst (wsj/create)\n        client-b-inst (wsj/create)\n\n        ;; Peer A accounting\n        a-deliveries (atom [])\n        a-delivered-once? (atom false)\n\n        ;; Server bind\n        server-bound? (atom false)\n        server-port (atom 0)\n        _ (wsj/bind! server-inst\n                     {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                      :bind-port 0\n                      :ws/max-frame-bytes 4194304\n                      :codecs [cbor/profile]\n                      :deposit! (fn [event detail]\n                                  (when (= :bind-succeeded event)\n                                    (reset! server-port (:port detail))\n                                    (reset! server-bound? true)))\n                      :accept! (fn [path seam _now]\n                                 (cond\n                                   (= path \"/peer-a\")\n                                   {:ws/adapter\n                                    {:opened! (fn [])\n                                     :binary! (fn [payload]\n                                                (swap! a-deliveries conj (alength payload)))\n                                     :message! (fn [_])\n                                     :closed! (fn [_ _])\n                                     :error! (fn [_])\n                                     :too-large! (fn [])}}\n\n                                   (= path \"/peer-b\")\n                                   (let [seam-ref (atom seam)]\n                                     {:ws/adapter\n                                      {:opened! (fn [])\n                                       :binary! (fn [payload]\n                                                  ((:send! @seam-ref) payload))\n                                       :message! (fn [msg]\n                                                   ((:send! @seam-ref) msg))\n                                       :closed! (fn [_ _])\n                                       :error! (fn [_])\n                                       :too-large! (fn [])}})))})\n\n        ;; Drive until server listener is bound\n        _ (loop []\n            (check-deadline! deadline :server-bind)\n            (when-not @server-bound?\n              (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump server-inst) nil)\n              (recur)))\n        port @server-port]\n\n    (println \"Server listening on port\" port)\n\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ;; Peer B setup: connects via client adapter\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    (let [b-opened? (atom false)\n          b-samples (atom [])\n          b-outstanding (atom {})\n          b-lost (atom 0)\n          b-req-seq (atom 0)\n          b-seam (atom nil)\n          b-cb {:ws/codec cbor/profile\n                :ws/max-frame-bytes 4194304\n                :opened! #(reset! b-opened? true)\n                :binary! (fn [payload]\n                           (let [now (poller/clock-ms)\n                                 req-id (bytes->int32 payload 0)\n                                 sent-at (get @b-outstanding req-id)]\n                             (if sent-at\n                               (do (swap! b-outstanding dissoc req-id)\n                                   (swap! b-samples conj (- now sent-at))\n                                   ;; The independent sampler observes every completed reply.\n                                   (println \"W-SAMPLE\" (- now sent-at))\n                                   (flush))\n                               (swap! b-lost inc))))\n                :message! (fn [_])\n                :closed! (fn [_ _])\n                :error! (fn [_])\n                :too-large! (fn [])}]\n\n      (reset! b-seam (wsj/connect! client-b-inst\n                                   {:ws/host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                                    :ws/port port\n                                    :ws/path \"/peer-b\"}\n                                   b-cb))\n\n      ;; Drive until Peer B connection is open\n      (loop []\n        (check-deadline! deadline :peer-b-upgrade)\n        (when-not @b-opened?\n          (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump server-inst) nil)\n          (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump client-b-inst) nil)\n          (recur)))\n\n      (println \"Peer B connected and upgraded\")\n\n      ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n      ;; Peer A setup: connects via raw socket, upgrades to /peer-a\n      ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n      (let [raw-a (raw-peer! port)\n            upgrade-req (str \"GET /peer-a HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\"\n                             \"Host: 127.0.0.1:\" port \"\\r\\n\"\n                             \"Upgrade: websocket\\r\\n\"\n                             \"Connection: Upgrade\\r\\n\"\n                             \"Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==\\r\\n\"\n                             \"Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13\\r\\n\"\n                             \"Sec-WebSocket-Protocol: dao.stream.cbor\\r\\n\\r\\n\")]\n        (write-all! (:fd raw-a) (text/utf8-encode upgrade-req) deadline)\n\n        ;; Drive until Peer A upgrade response arrives\n        (loop []\n          (check-deadline! deadline :peer-a-upgrade)\n          (raw-poll! raw-a)\n          (when-not (head-end @(:in raw-a))\n            (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump server-inst) nil)\n            (recur)))\n\n        (println \"Peer A connected and upgraded\")\n\n        ;; Prepare Peer A's 4,194,304-byte binary frame\n        (let [a-payload (bytes/alloc 4194304)\n              mask-key (byte-array [(unchecked-byte 0x37) (unchecked-byte 0xFA)\n                                    (unchecked-byte 0x21) (unchecked-byte 0x3D)])\n              a-frame (rfc6455/encode-frame {:opcode :binary\n                                             :payload a-payload\n                                             :mask-key mask-key\n                                             :fin? true})\n              total-a-frame-len (alength a-frame)\n              a-sent (atom 0)\n\n              ;; REPL loop setup\n              repl-state (atom (repl.main/boot {:adapter (host/websocket)}))\n              repl-step-count (atom 0)\n              owner-round-count (atom 0)\n\n              ;; Timing and dispatch state\n              start-time (poller/clock-ms)\n              last-10hz-tick (atom start-time)\n              last-telemetry-tick (atom start-time)\n              a-stalls (atom 0)\n              a-stalled? (atom false)\n              timed-out? (atom false)\n              max-consecutive-stalls 500\n              server-pump (wsj/host-pump server-inst)\n              client-b-pump (wsj/host-pump client-b-inst)]\n\n          (println \"Frame length for Peer A:\" total-a-frame-len \"bytes\")\n          (println \"Entering owner round loop...\")\n          (flush)\n\n          ;; Main owner loop\n          (loop []\n            (let [now (poller/clock-ms)\n                  elapsed (- now start-time)]\n\n              ;; Hard timeout guard: cap whole run at 5 minutes\n              (if (>= now deadline)\n                (do (reset! timed-out? true)\n                    (println \"HARD CAP EXCEEDED: workload-w exceeded 5 minutes (\"\n                             (- now run-start) \"ms), terminating loop.\"))\n\n                (do\n                  ;; 1. Advance owner round and step REPL loop\n                  (swap! owner-round-count inc)\n                  (when (= 1 @owner-round-count)\n                    (println \"W-PHASE repl-step\")\n                    (flush))\n                  (driver/submit-line! (:input @repl-state)\n                                       \"(loop [i 0] (if (< i 5) (recur (inc i)) i))\")\n                  (let [stepped (driver/repl-step @repl-state now)\n                        [_entries next-st] (driver/take-outbox stepped)]\n                    (reset! repl-state next-st)\n                    (swap! repl-step-count inc))\n\n                  ;; 2. Paced Peer A transfer across target-duration-ms (with no-progress guard)\n                  (when (= 1 @owner-round-count)\n                    (println \"W-PHASE peer-a-send\")\n                    (flush))\n                  (when-not @a-stalled?\n                    (let [target-bytes (if (>= elapsed target-duration-ms)\n                                         total-a-frame-len\n                                         (min total-a-frame-len\n                                              (long (* total-a-frame-len\n                                                       (/ (double elapsed) (double target-duration-ms))))))]\n                      (when (< @a-sent target-bytes)\n                        (let [to-write (min (- target-bytes @a-sent) 65536)\n                              r (write-some! (:fd raw-a) a-frame @a-sent to-write)]\n                          (cond\n                            (or (= :would-block r) (= 0 r))\n                            (let [stalls (swap! a-stalls inc)]\n                              (native/poll-one (:fd raw-a) native/pollout 0)\n                              (when (>= stalls max-consecutive-stalls)\n                                (reset! a-stalled? true)\n                                (println \"TRANSFER STALLED: Peer A hit\" stalls\n                                         \"consecutive no-progress polls at\" @a-sent \"/\" total-a-frame-len \"bytes. Aborting transfer leg.\")\n                                (flush)))\n\n                            (and (number? r) (pos? r))\n                            (do (reset! a-stalls 0)\n                                (swap! a-sent + r))\n\n                            :else\n                            (do (reset! a-stalled? true)\n                                (println \"TRANSFER STALLED: unexpected Peer A send result\" r)\n                                (flush)))))))\n\n                  ;; 3. Peer B 10 Hz dispatch: concurrent requests up to 8 outstanding\n                  (when (>= (- now @last-10hz-tick) 100)\n                    (reset! last-10hz-tick now)\n                    (when (< (count @b-outstanding) 8)\n                      (let [id (swap! b-req-seq inc)\n                            req-bs (make-1kib-request id)]\n                        (swap! b-outstanding assoc id now)\n                        ((:send! @b-seam) req-bs))))\n\n                  ;; 4. Periodic sampler flush every 10 seconds to ensure telemetry\n                  (when (>= (- now @last-telemetry-tick) 10000)\n                    (reset! last-telemetry-tick now)\n                    (let [sc (count @b-samples)\n                          cur-p99 (when (pos? sc)\n                                    (nth (sort @b-samples) (min (dec sc) (int (* 0.99 sc)))))]\n                      (println (str \"TELEMETRY: elapsed=\" (long (/ elapsed 1000)) \"s, samples=\" sc\n                                    \", running p99=\" (if cur-p99 (str cur-p99 \"ms\") \"ABSENT\") \", A-sent=\" @a-sent \"/\" total-a-frame-len\n                                    \" (\" (long (* 100 (/ (double @a-sent) (double total-a-frame-len)))) \"%), A-deliveries=\" (count @a-deliveries)\n                                    \", index-cursor=\" (pr-str (get-in @repl-state [:repl :indexer :materialization :cursor]))\n                                    (when @a-stalled? \" [A-STALLED]\")))\n                      (flush)))\n\n                  ;; 5. Pump I/O for server and client B\n                  (when (= 1 @owner-round-count)\n                    (println \"W-PHASE server-pump\")\n                    (flush))\n                  (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 server-pump nil)\n                  (when (= 1 @owner-round-count)\n                    (println \"W-PHASE client-b-pump\")\n                    (flush))\n                  (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 client-b-pump nil)\n                  (when (= 1 @owner-round-count)\n                    (println \"W-PHASE owner-round-complete\")\n                    (flush))\n\n                  ;; Check exit conditions\n                  (let [a-done? (or @a-stalled?\n                                    (and (>= @a-sent total-a-frame-len)\n                                         (seq @a-deliveries)))\n                        time-done? (>= elapsed target-duration-ms)\n                        samples-done? (>= (count @b-samples) target-samples)]\n\n                    (if (and a-done? time-done? samples-done?)\n                      ;; Drain trailing in-flight requests briefly\n                      (let [drain-deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) 500)]\n                        (loop []\n                          (when (and (seq @b-outstanding)\n                                     (< (poller/clock-ms) (min deadline drain-deadline)))\n                            (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 server-pump nil)\n                            (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 client-b-pump nil)\n                            (recur))))\n                      (recur)))))))\n\n          ;; Cleanup sockets\n          (raw-close! raw-a)\n          (wsj/close-host! client-b-inst)\n          (wsj/close-host! server-inst)\n          (wake/close! w)\n\n          ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------\n          ;; Evaluation of pre-registered criterion\n          ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------\n          (let [sample-count (count @b-samples)\n                elapsed-ms (- (poller/clock-ms) run-start)\n                valid-run? (>= sample-count 500)\n                target-samples-met? (>= sample-count target-samples)\n                sorted (sort @b-samples)\n                p99-idx (int (* 0.99 sample-count))\n                p99 (when (and (not @timed-out?) (pos? sample-count))\n                      (nth sorted (min (dec sample-count) p99-idx)))\n                p99-ok? (and (some? p99) (<= p99 250))\n                repl-advances? (= @owner-round-count @repl-step-count)\n                a-deliv-count (count @a-deliveries)\n                a-deliv-len (or (first @a-deliveries) 0)\n                delivered-once? (and (= 1 a-deliv-count)\n                                     (= 4194304 a-deliv-len))\n                replies-lost (count @b-outstanding)\n                no-reply-lost? (and (zero? @b-lost) (zero? replies-lost))\n                all-pass? (and (not @timed-out?)\n                               (not @a-stalled?)\n                               valid-run?\n                               target-samples-met?\n                               p99-ok?\n                               repl-advances?\n                               delivered-once?\n                               no-reply-lost?)]\n\n            (println)\n            (println \"================================================================================\")\n            (println \"Workload W Results:\" (if all-pass? \"SUCCESS\" \"FAILURE\"))\n            (println \"================================================================================\")\n            (println \"  Hard cap timed out:           \" @timed-out?)\n            (println \"  Elapsed:                      \" elapsed-ms \"ms\")\n            (println \"  Peer A transfer stalled:      \" @a-stalled?)\n            (println \"  Sample count:                 \" sample-count\n                     (str \"(valid >= 500: \" valid-run? \", target >= \" target-samples \": \" target-samples-met? \")\"))\n            (println \"  p99 round-trip latency:       \" (if (some? p99) (str p99 \"ms\") \"ABSENT\")\n                     (str \"(criterion <= 250 ms: \" p99-ok? \")\"))\n            (println \"  Peer B replies lost:          \" replies-lost \"(unanswered) +\" @b-lost \"(corrupt)\")\n            (println \"  Peer A delivered once:        \" delivered-once?\n                     (str \"(deliveries: \" a-deliv-count \", bytes: \" a-deliv-len \", sent: \" @a-sent \")\"))\n            (println \"  REPL per-round step advance:  \" repl-advances?\n                     (str \"(rounds: \" @owner-round-count \", REPL steps: \" @repl-step-count \")\"))\n            (println)\n\n            ;; Required report records per BRIEF-j6g.md\n            (if (some? p99)\n              (println \"RECORD p99\" p99 \"ms\")\n              (println \"RECORD p99 ABSENT\"))\n            (println \"RECORD sample count\" sample-count)\n            (println \"RECORD elapsed ms\" elapsed-ms)\n            (println \"RECORD delivered once\" delivered-once?)\n            (println \"RECORD per-round step advance\" repl-advances?)\n\n            (if all-pass?\n              (do (println \"PASS workload-w\")\n                  {:pass? true\n                   :p99 p99\n                   :samples sample-count\n                   :elapsed-ms elapsed-ms\n                   :timed-out? @timed-out?\n                   :transfer-stalled? @a-stalled?\n                   :delivered-once? delivered-once?\n                   :repl-advances? repl-advances?\n                   :replies-lost replies-lost})\n              (do (println \"FAIL workload-w\")\n                  {:pass? false\n                   :p99 p99\n                   :samples sample-count\n                   :elapsed-ms elapsed-ms\n                   :timed-out? @timed-out?\n                   :transfer-stalled? @a-stalled?\n                   :delivered-once? delivered-once?\n                   :repl-advances? repl-advances?\n                   :replies-lost replies-lost}))))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Deliverable 2: Peer Cross-Host Rows\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn run-cross-host-rows\n  \"Verify cross-host listener, dialer, and REPL path reachability rows:\n   - Hosts that listen: 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, localhost, ::1; port 0 ephemeral; port collision.\n   - Hosts that dial: 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1]; hostname refused; wss refused.\n   - REPL reachability refusals: local reachability failure, reachability failure, disclaimed path.\"\n  []\n  (println \"================================================================================\")\n  (println \"J6g Cross-Host Rows: Running checks...\")\n  (println \"================================================================================\")\n  (let [w (wake/make-wake)\n        passed (atom 0)\n        failed (atom 0)\n        check! (fn [label ok? detail]\n                 (if ok?\n                   (do (swap! passed inc)\n                       (println \"  ok  \" label))\n                   (do (swap! failed inc)\n                       (println \"  FAIL\" label detail))))]\n\n    ;; 1. Hosts that listen: 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, localhost, ::1\n    (doseq [bind-host [\"127.0.0.1\" \"0.0.0.0\" \"localhost\" \"::1\"]]\n      (let [inst (wsj/create)\n            bound-port (atom nil)\n            res (wsj/bind! inst {:bind-host bind-host\n                                 :bind-port 0\n                                 :deposit! (fn [ev detail]\n                                             (when (= :bind-succeeded ev)\n                                               (reset! bound-port (:port detail))))})]\n        (check! (str \"listen on \" bind-host \" binds ephemeral port\")\n                (and (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome res))\n                     (pos? (or @bound-port 0)))\n                {:res res :port @bound-port})\n        (wsj/close-host! inst)))\n\n    ;; Port collision check\n    (let [inst1 (wsj/create)\n          inst2 (wsj/create)\n          bound-port (atom nil)\n          _ (wsj/bind! inst1 {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                              :bind-port 0\n                              :deposit! (fn [ev detail]\n                                          (when (= :bind-succeeded ev)\n                                            (reset! bound-port (:port detail))))})\n          collision-res (wsj/bind! inst2 {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                                          :bind-port @bound-port\n                                          :deposit! (fn [_ _])})]\n      (check! \"listen on colliding port returns transport-error\"\n              (= :dao.stream/transport-error (:dao.stream/outcome collision-res))\n              {:collision collision-res})\n      (wsj/close-host! inst2)\n      (wsj/close-host! inst1))\n\n    ;; 2. Hosts that dial: 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1]\n    (doseq [[bind-h dial-h label] [[\"127.0.0.1\" \"127.0.0.1\" \"127.0.0.1\"]\n                                   [\"127.0.0.1\" \"localhost\" \"localhost\"]\n                                   [\"::1\" \"[::1]\" \"[::1]\"]]]\n      (let [si (wsj/create)\n            ci (wsj/create)\n            port (atom nil)\n            _ (wsj/bind! si {:bind-host bind-h\n                             :bind-port 0\n                             :deposit! (fn [ev detail]\n                                         (when (= :bind-succeeded ev)\n                                           (reset! port (:port detail))))})\n            opened? (atom false)\n            seam (wsj/connect! ci\n                               {:ws/host dial-h :ws/port @port :ws/path \"/repl\"}\n                               {:ws/codec cbor/profile\n                                :opened! #(reset! opened? true)\n                                :binary! (fn [_])\n                                :message! (fn [_])\n                                :closed! (fn [_ _])\n                                :error! (fn [_])\n                                :too-large! (fn [])})\n            deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) 5000)]\n        (loop []\n          (when-not (or @opened? (> (poller/clock-ms) deadline))\n            (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n            (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump ci) nil)\n            (recur)))\n        (check! (str \"dial on \" label \" connects and opens\")\n                (and (some? seam) @opened?)\n                {:seam seam :opened? @opened?})\n        (wsj/close-host! ci)\n        (wsj/close-host! si)))\n\n    ;; Refusal of host name (resolver trap not sprung)\n    (let [ci (wsj/create)\n          trap (fn [& _] (throw (ex-info \"resolver called\" {})))]\n      (with-redefs-fn {#'native/resolve-addrs trap\n                       #'native/c-getaddrinfo trap}\n        (fn []\n          (let [seam (wsj/connect! ci\n                                   {:ws/host \"example.invalid\"\n                                    :ws/port 80\n                                    :ws/path \"/repl\"}\n                                   {:opened! (fn [])\n                                    :closed! (fn [_ _])\n                                    :message! (fn [_])\n                                    :binary! (fn [_])})]\n            (check! \"dial to hostname returns nil, resolver trap not sprung\"\n                    (nil? seam)\n                    {:seam seam}))))\n      (wsj/close-host! ci))\n\n    ;; Refusal of wss scheme\n    (let [ci (wsj/create)\n          seam (wsj/connect! ci\n                             {:ws/scheme \"wss\"\n                              :ws/host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                              :ws/port 443\n                              :ws/path \"/repl\"}\n                             {:opened! (fn [])\n                              :closed! (fn [_ _])\n                              :message! (fn [_])\n                              :binary! (fn [_])})]\n      (check! \"dial with wss scheme is refused explicitly (nil returned)\"\n              (nil? seam)\n              {:seam seam})\n      (wsj/close-host! ci))\n\n    ;; 3. Reachability refusals on the REPL path (yin.repl.connect/open)\n    ;; 3a. Host name yields local reachability failure text\n    (let [ci (wsj/create)\n          opened (connect/open {:url \"daostream:ws://example.invalid:80/repl\"\n                                :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))}\n                                :now 0})\n          text (pr-str opened)]\n      (check! \"connect/open to hostname gives local reachability failure text\"\n              (str/includes? text \"local reachability failure\")\n              {:opened text})\n      (wsj/close-host! ci))\n\n    ;; 3b. Unbound / unreachable port yields reachability failure text\n    (let [ci (wsj/create)\n          ;; Unbound port on loopback: dial fails immediately on connect\n          unused-port 59999\n          opened (connect/open {:url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" unused-port \"/repl\")\n                                :host (host/websocket)\n                                :now 0})\n          conn (connect/connection-key opened)\n          client (connect/client-key opened)\n          deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) 3000)\n          observed (loop [c conn cl client]\n                     (let [c' (connect/step! c (poller/clock-ms))\n                           cl' (:dao.stream.rpc/state (rpc/poll! cl))\n                           [c'' ev] (connect/observe-terminal c' (:terminal cl'))]\n                       (cond\n                         ev [c'' ev]\n                         (> (poller/clock-ms) deadline) [c'' nil]\n                         :else (do (wake/sleep-fds! w 10 nil nil)\n                                   (recur c'' cl')))))\n          event (second observed)\n          ev-text (connect/text-key event)]\n      (check! \"connect/open to unbound port observes transport-error reachability failure\"\n              (and (some? event)\n                   (str/includes? (str ev-text) \"reachability failure\"))\n              {:event event :text ev-text})\n      (wsj/close-host! ci))\n\n    (wake/close! w)\n    (println)\n    (println \"Cross-host rows:\" @passed \"passed,\" @failed \"failed\")\n    (if (zero? @failed)\n      (do (println \"PASS cross-host\") true)\n      (do (println \"FAIL cross-host\") false))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Deliverable 3: REPL Served-Stream Rejoin Checks\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- step-peer!\n  [peer now]\n  (swap! (:connection peer) connect/step! now)\n  (swap! (:client peer) #(:dao.stream.rpc/state (rpc/poll! %))))\n\n\n(defn- drive-repl-peers!\n  [w insts peers pred ms]\n  (let [deadline (+ (poller/clock-ms) ms)\n        hps (mapv wsj/host-pump insts)]\n    (loop []\n      (let [now (poller/clock-ms)]\n        (doseq [p peers] (step-peer! p now))\n        (doseq [hp hps] (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 hp nil))\n        (cond\n          (pred) true\n          (> (poller/clock-ms) deadline) false\n          :else (recur))))))\n\n\n(defn run-served-stream-rejoin\n  \"Run the 4 served-stream facts over two jolt host instances:\n   Fact 1: evaluation round trips and two clients get their own answers.\n   Fact 2: killing connection is observable and requests are lost.\n   Fact 3: reattaching resumes served stream and deposit medium.\n   Fact 4: stopping endpoint ends served stream (not closes it).\"\n  []\n  (println \"================================================================================\")\n  (println \"J6g Served-Stream Rejoin: Running 4 served-stream peer facts...\")\n  (println \"================================================================================\")\n  (let [w (wake/make-wake)\n        passed (atom 0)\n        failed (atom 0)\n        check! (fn [label ok? detail]\n                 (if ok?\n                   (do (swap! passed inc)\n                       (println \"  ok  \" label))\n                   (do (swap! failed inc)\n                       (println \"  FAIL\" label detail))))]\n\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ;; Fact 1: Two clients connect and get their own answers without crosstalk\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    (let [si (wsj/create)\n          ci1 (wsj/create)\n          ci2 (wsj/create)\n          port (atom 0)\n          server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                                         :bind-port 0\n                                         :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n                                                         (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n                                                                              #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n                                                :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n                                                :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n          ;; Advance server until port bound\n          _ (loop []\n              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                (reset! server-st next-st)\n                (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n                  (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n                (when (zero? @port)\n                  (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n                  (recur))))\n          url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n          now (poller/clock-ms)\n          p1-open (connect/open {:url url :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci1 d a))} :now now})\n          p2-open (connect/open {:url url :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci2 d a))} :now now})\n          peer1 {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p1-open))\n                 :client (atom (connect/client-key p1-open))}\n          peer2 {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p2-open))\n                 :client (atom (connect/client-key p2-open))}]\n\n      ;; Send eval requests\n      (let [c1 (rpc/request! @(:client peer1) 1 :op/eval \"(+ 40 2)\")\n            c2 (rpc/request! @(:client peer2) 2 :op/eval \"(+ 20 3)\")]\n        (reset! (:client peer1) c1)\n        (reset! (:client peer2) c2)\n\n        ;; Drive until both answers arrive\n        (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci1 ci2] [peer1 peer2]\n                           (fn []\n                             (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                                   [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                               (reset! server-st next-st)\n                               (and (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer1) 1))\n                                    (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer2) 2)))))\n                           5000)\n\n        (let [ans1 (rpc/response @(:client peer1) 1)\n              ans2 (rpc/response @(:client peer2) 2)]\n          (check! \"Fact 1: peer 1 gets 42 and peer 2 gets 23 without crosstalk\"\n                  (and (= \"42\" (rpc/answer-ok ans1))\n                       (= \"23\" (rpc/answer-ok ans2))\n                       (nil? (rpc/response @(:client peer1) 2))\n                       (nil? (rpc/response @(:client peer2) 1)))\n                  {:ans1 ans1 :ans2 ans2})))\n\n      (wsj/close-host! ci1)\n      (wsj/close-host! ci2)\n      (wsj/close-host! si))\n\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ;; Fact 2: Killing connection while request in-flight observes request lost\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    (let [si (wsj/create)\n          ci (wsj/create)\n          port (atom 0)\n          server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                                         :bind-port 0\n                                         :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n                                                         (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n                                                                              #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n                                                :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n                                                :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n          _ (loop []\n              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                (reset! server-st next-st)\n                (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n                  (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n                (when (zero? @port)\n                  (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n                  (recur))))\n          url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n          now (poller/clock-ms)\n          p-open (connect/open {:url url :host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))} :now now})\n          peer {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p-open))\n                :client (atom (connect/client-key p-open))}]\n\n      ;; Connect first\n      (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n                         (fn []\n                           (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                                 [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                             (reset! server-st next-st)\n                             (remote-channel/opened? (:dial @(:connection peer)))))\n                         3000)\n\n      ;; Stall server driver, send request, and close client connection\n      (let [c (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 99 :op/eval \"(+ 1 2)\")]\n        (reset! (:client peer) c)\n        (swap! (:connection peer) connect/close!)\n\n        ;; Drive client until terminal observed\n        (drive-repl-peers! w [ci] [peer]\n                           (fn []\n                             (some? (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n                           3000)\n\n        (let [[conn' ev] (connect/observe-terminal @(:connection peer) (:terminal @(:client peer)))]\n          (check! \"Fact 2: client observes detached and lost request\"\n                  (and (= :detached (:status conn'))\n                       (= :dao.stream.rpc/detached (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n                  {:conn conn' :ev ev :terminal (:terminal @(:client peer))})))\n\n      (wsj/close-host! ci)\n      (wsj/close-host! si))\n\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ;; Fact 3: Reattaching resumes served stream and shared shell state survives\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    (let [si (wsj/create)\n          ci (wsj/create)\n          port (atom 0)\n          server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                                         :bind-port 0\n                                         :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n                                                         (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n                                                                              #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n                                                :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n                                                :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n          _ (loop []\n              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                (reset! server-st next-st)\n                (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n                  (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n                (when (zero? @port)\n                  (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n                  (recur))))\n          url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n          now (poller/clock-ms)\n          client-host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))}\n          p-open (connect/open {:url url :host client-host :now now})\n          peer {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p-open))\n                :client (atom (connect/client-key p-open))}]\n\n      ;; 1. Define x = 10\n      (let [c (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 10 :op/eval \"(def x 10)\")]\n        (reset! (:client peer) c)\n        (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n                           (fn []\n                             (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                                   [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                               (reset! server-st next-st)\n                               (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer) 10))))\n                           5000)\n        (check! \"Fact 3: (def x 10) evaluated\"\n                (= \"10\" (rpc/answer-ok (rpc/response @(:client peer) 10)))\n                {:ans (rpc/response @(:client peer) 10)}))\n\n      ;; 2. Disconnect and reattach\n      (swap! (:connection peer) connect/close!)\n      (drive-repl-peers! w [ci] [peer]\n                         (fn [] (some? (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n                         2000)\n      (let [now' (poller/clock-ms)\n            reattached (connect/reattach @(:connection peer) @(:client peer) now')]\n        (reset! (:connection peer) (connect/connection-key reattached))\n        (reset! (:client peer) (connect/client-key reattached))\n\n        ;; 3. Query x on reattached connection\n        (let [c2 (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 11 :op/eval \"x\")]\n          (reset! (:client peer) c2)\n          (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n                             (fn []\n                               (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                                     [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                                 (reset! server-st next-st)\n                                 (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer) 11))))\n                             5000)\n          (check! \"Fact 3: reattached connection reads x = 10\"\n                  (= \"10\" (rpc/answer-ok (rpc/response @(:client peer) 11)))\n                  {:ans (rpc/response @(:client peer) 11)})))\n\n      (wsj/close-host! ci)\n      (wsj/close-host! si))\n\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ;; Fact 4: Stopping endpoint ends served stream (terminal :ended, not :detached)\n    ;; -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    (let [si (wsj/create)\n          ci (wsj/create)\n          port (atom 0)\n          server-st (atom (serve/serve! {:bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n                                         :bind-port 0\n                                         :host {:bind! (fn [opts]\n                                                         (wsj/bind! si (assoc opts :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n                                                                              #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n                                                :unbind! (fn [lh dep] (wsj/unbind! si lh dep))\n                                                :connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! si d a))}}))\n          _ (loop []\n              (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                    [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                (reset! server-st next-st)\n                (when-let [p (get-in next-st [:spec :port])]\n                  (when (pos? p) (reset! port p)))\n                (when (zero? @port)\n                  (wake/sleep-fds! w 5 (wsj/host-pump si) nil)\n                  (recur))))\n          url (str \"daostream:ws://127.0.0.1:\" @port \"/repl\")\n          now (poller/clock-ms)\n          client-host {:connect! (fn [d a] (wsj/connect! ci d a))}\n          p-open (connect/open {:url url :host client-host :now now})\n          peer {:connection (atom (connect/connection-key p-open))\n                :client (atom (connect/client-key p-open))}]\n\n      ;; Open connection and eval (+ 3 4)\n      (let [c (rpc/request! @(:client peer) 7 :op/eval \"(+ 3 4)\")]\n        (reset! (:client peer) c)\n        (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n                           (fn []\n                             (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                                   [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                               (reset! server-st next-st)\n                               (some? (rpc/response @(:client peer) 7))))\n                           5000)\n        (check! \"Fact 4: (+ 3 4) evaluates to 7\"\n                (= \"7\" (rpc/answer-ok (rpc/response @(:client peer) 7)))\n                {:ans (rpc/response @(:client peer) 7)}))\n\n      ;; Stop endpoint\n      (swap! server-st serve/stop!)\n      (drive-repl-peers! w [si ci] [peer]\n                         (fn []\n                           (let [stepped (serve/step @server-st (poller/clock-ms))\n                                 [_ next-st] (serve/take-outbox stepped)]\n                             (reset! server-st next-st)\n                             (and (serve/stopped? next-st)\n                                  (some? (:terminal @(:client peer))))))\n                         5000)\n\n      (let [[conn' ev] (connect/observe-terminal @(:connection peer) (:terminal @(:client peer)))]\n        (check! \"Fact 4: client observes :ended, not bare detached\"\n                (and (= :ended (:status conn'))\n                     (= :dao.stream.rpc/ended (:terminal @(:client peer))))\n                {:conn conn' :ev ev :terminal (:terminal @(:client peer))}))\n\n      (wsj/close-host! ci)\n      (wsj/close-host! si))\n\n    (wake/close! w)\n    (println)\n    (println \"Served-stream rejoin:\" @passed \"passed,\" @failed \"failed\")\n    (if (zero? @failed)\n      (do (println \"PASS served-stream\") true)\n      (do (println \"FAIL served-stream\") false))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; CLI Dispatcher\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn -main\n  [& args]\n  (when-not (= \"1\" (System/getenv \"JOLT_WORKLOAD_W_SUPERVISED\"))\n    (println \"Workload W Results: FAILURE\")\n    (println \"Use bb workload-w:jolt so the independent watchdog owns the runtime cap.\")\n    (println \"RECORD p99 ABSENT\")\n    (println \"RECORD sample count 0\")\n    (println \"RECORD elapsed ms 0\")\n    (println \"FAIL workload-w\")\n    (flush)\n    (System/exit 1))\n  (let [cmd (or (first args) \"workload-w\")\n        ok? (case cmd\n              \"workload-w\" (:pass? (run-workload-w))\n              \"quick-w\" (:pass? (run-workload-w :target-duration-ms 2000 :target-samples 20))\n              \"cross-host\" (run-cross-host-rows)\n              \"served-stream\" (run-served-stream-rejoin)\n              \"all\" (and (run-cross-host-rows)\n                         (run-served-stream-rejoin)\n                         (:pass? (run-workload-w)))\n              (do (println \"Unknown command:\" cmd\n                           \"- valid commands: workload-w, quick-w, cross-host, served-stream, all\")\n                  false))]\n    (System/exit (if ok? 0 1))))\n\n\n(apply -main *command-line-args*)\n(ns jolt-workload-w-run\n  \"Independent watchdog and sampler for the single-threaded Workload W worker.\n   Run with bb workload-w:jolt [workload-w|quick-w|cross-host|served-stream|all].\"\n  (:require [clojure.edn :as edn]\n            [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n\n(def hard-cap-ms 300000)\n(def telemetry-ms 10000)\n\n\n(defn- now-ms\n  []\n  (quot (System/nanoTime) 1000000))\n\n\n(defn- p99\n  [samples]\n  (when (seq samples)\n    (nth (sort samples) (min (dec (count samples)) (int (* 0.99 (count samples)))))))\n\n\n(defn- observe-line\n  [state line]\n  (if (str/starts-with? line \"W-SAMPLE \")\n    (let [sample (edn/read-string (subs line 9))]\n      (when-not (and (number? sample) (<= 0 sample))\n        (throw (ex-info \"Invalid worker sample\" {:line line})))\n      (-> state\n          (update :samples conj sample)\n          (assoc :last-output-at (now-ms))))\n    (do\n      (println line)\n      (flush)\n      (cond-> (assoc state :last-output-at (now-ms))\n        (str/starts-with? line \"W-PHASE \") (assoc :phase (subs line 8))\n        (= line \"FAIL workload-w\") (assoc :reported-failure? true)))))\n\n\n(defn- read-output\n  \"Only ready characters, at most 8192 per turn: a partial line or a noisy\n   worker must not block the watchdog's clock or its ten-second sampler.\"\n  [reader state]\n  (let [chunk (StringBuilder.)]\n    (loop [remaining 8192]\n      (when (and (pos? remaining) (.ready reader))\n        (let [c (.read reader)]\n          (when (not= -1 c)\n            (.append chunk (char c))\n            (recur (dec remaining))))))\n    (let [lines (str/split (str (:partial state) chunk) #\"\\n\" -1)]\n      (assoc (reduce observe-line state (butlast lines)) :partial (last lines)))))\n\n\n(defn- telemetry!\n  [state elapsed]\n  (println \"WATCHDOG TELEMETRY\"\n           (pr-str {:elapsed-ms elapsed,\n                    :samples (count (:samples state)),\n                    :running-p99-ms (p99 (:samples state)),\n                    :first-round-phase (:phase state),\n                    :output-age-ms (- (now-ms) (:last-output-at state))}))\n  (flush))\n\n\n(defn- failure!\n  [state elapsed reason]\n  (println \"Workload W Results: FAILURE\")\n  (println \"RECORD failure reason\" reason)\n  (println \"RECORD p99 ABSENT\")\n  (println \"RECORD sample count\" (count (:samples state)))\n  (println \"RECORD elapsed ms\" elapsed)\n  (println \"FAIL workload-w\")\n  (flush))\n\n\n(defn- run-watched\n  [args]\n  (let [started (now-ms)\n        command (into [\"sh\" \"-c\"\n                       \". build/tools/jolt-env.sh && exec \\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\" -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj \\\"$@\\\"\"\n                       \"jolt\"] args)\n        builder (ProcessBuilder. ^java.util.List command)\n        _ (.put (.environment builder) \"JOLT_WORKLOAD_W_SUPERVISED\" \"1\")\n        _ (.redirectError builder java.lang.ProcessBuilder$Redirect/INHERIT)\n        process (.start builder)]\n    (try\n      (with-open [reader (java.io.BufferedReader.\n                          (java.io.InputStreamReader. (.getInputStream process) \"UTF-8\"))]\n        (loop [state {:samples [], :partial \"\", :phase \"loading\", :last-output-at started}\n               next-telemetry (+ started telemetry-ms)]\n          (let [state (read-output reader state)\n                now (now-ms)\n                elapsed (- now started)]\n            (when (>= now next-telemetry)\n              (telemetry! state elapsed))\n            (cond\n              (>= elapsed hard-cap-ms)\n              (do\n                ;; Kill directly: the shell execs jolt, so no worker is left behind.\n                (.destroyForcibly process)\n                (failure! state elapsed :hard-cap)\n                1)\n\n              (not (.isAlive process))\n              ;; Drain already-buffered output without waiting for another line.\n              (if (.ready reader)\n                (recur state next-telemetry)\n                (let [state (if (str/blank? (:partial state))\n                              state\n                              (observe-line state (:partial state)))\n                      status (.exitValue process)]\n                  (when (and (not (zero? status)) (not (:reported-failure? state)))\n                    (failure! state elapsed :worker-failure))\n                  status))\n\n              :else\n              (do\n                (Thread/sleep 10)\n                (recur state (if (>= now next-telemetry)\n                               (+ now telemetry-ms)\n                               next-telemetry)))))))\n      (finally\n        (when (.isAlive process) (.destroyForcibly process))))))\n\n\n(System/exit (run-watched *command-line-args*))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
