correction), and what you could not verify.\ncollab/jolt/1792458308000-reviewer-jolt-j0a-baseline.qwen3.8-max.prompt.md:13:Output (under 600 words): verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, a findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction) and what you could not verify. Put the whole answer in your final response (you cannot write files); include the header fields at the top.\ncollab/jolt/1793145000000-architect-j6a-signoff.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:16:- **P2 | docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:530 | evidence:** the accepted set stops at 1011, so inbound 1012 to 1014 are answered 1002. The same document has served connections send 1013 for pending-overflow at line 321, and the JVM-client row at line 528 says served connections are the only sender of 1013. A jolt client attached to a JVM or Node server would therefore treat a legitimate overflow close from a sibling host as a protocol error. Nothing forces this: at the pinned release the 7.9.x rows probe 0, 999, 1004 to 1006, 1016, 1100, 2000 and 2999, never 1012 to 1014, and §9.5 line 1282 lists the invalid codes as below 1000, 1004 to 1006 and 1015 to 2999. The engineer transcribed my verdict exactly. The defect is in the verdict's set, and I amend it here as Architect. **Fix:** accepted set becomes 1000 to 1003, 1007 to 1014, and 3000 to 4999. Make the parenthetical exhaustive: 0 to 999, 1004 to 1006, 1015 to 2999, and 5000 and above. No evaluator, exception row or hash depends on it.\ncollab/jolt/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity.claude-fable-5-1.prompt.md:10:Context. Owner decisions (verbatim): Q3 \"Q3 is not relevant if we use FFI with antlr C++ generated parser\" and \"no fall back\" (in-process ANTLR4 C++ parser over FFI; if the probe fails the orchestrator returns to the owner; no fallback parser). The design (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md section 7.8) requires jolt's exporter packets to be \"canonical CST/diagnostic packet equality with the JVM/Node/Dart oracle for identical source/unit/profile, then identical completeness-probe results\". J0c round 2 report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md (the orchestrator re-ran it: 51 corpus entries, 46 packets EQUAL, 5 DIVERGE, all in syntax-error records; all 51 completeness results (:end/:early/nil) and all 40 probe answers MATCH). Worktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c (uncommitted round-2 work; read-only for you). Divergences, per the report: (1) C++ grammar helper takes INDENT/EOF line and column from the previous token and never sets the DEDENT line (entries \"\\n\\n  \\n\", \"  x = 1\\n\", \"if x:\\n    @d\\ny = 1\\n\"): closable with a two-line helper patch in antlr/python3/manifest.edn (an experiment already took the divergences from 5 to 3); (2) C++ 4.13.2 runtime DefaultErrorStrategy differs from the Java one: empty token text vs null text (message \"''\" vs \"'<2>'\"), and C++ lacks the state Java records in sync() and uses in recoverInline, so the \"expecting\" set differs (entry \"d = {'a': [1, 2\\n\") and one case yields 2 errors instead of 3 (\"foo(1 2\\n\"). Closing (2) needs error-reporting changes below the grammar (a ported error strategy in the wrapper, or a runtime patch). The report also notes the existing repo tests ALREADY accept different helper-token coordinates on Node and Dart, while the round-2 brief asked for equality with the JVM.\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.brief.md:19:  the 5-minute hard cap with `:hard-cap`, `p99 ABSENT`, `sample count 70`,\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.brief.md:20:  A's transfer stalled. B's round trips starve (70 samples vs ≥600)\ncollab/jolt/1792454059000-architect-jolt-design-points.claude-fable-5-1.prompt.md:18:Write ONLY: the design document (edit in place) and your rationale file /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792454059000-architect-jolt-design-points.claude-fable-5-1.f