{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"web_search","id":"exec-58b1f3f6-aa75-4fe5-8c33-e48a1c45001a","query":"site.itl.nist.gov binomial proportion zero failures upper confidence limit ...","action":{"type":"search","queries":["site.itl.nist.gov binomial proportion zero failures upper confidence limit","site.hdrhistogram.org coordinated omission latency"]},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.itl.nist.gov","ref_id":"turn0search0","snippet":"Compute the exact binomial confidence limits for a binomial proportion. ... <upplim> is a variable that contains the computed upper exact binomial confidence limit; ...","title":"EXACT BINOMIAL CONFIDENCE LIMITS","url":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/software/dataplot/refman2/auxillar/exacbici.htm"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"github.com","ref_id":"turn0search1","snippet":"Four reproducible ways JVM latency measurements mislead — coordinated omission, warm-up, means, and hot-path allocation — with committed HdrHistogram receipts.","title":"GitHub - mrapaul/jvm-latency-receipts: Four reproducible ways JVM latency measurements mislead — coordinated omission, warm-up, means, and hot-path allocation — with committed HdrHistogram receipts. · GitHub","url":"https://github.com/mrapaul/jvm-latency-receipts"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.itl.nist.gov","ref_id":"turn0search12","snippet":"A one-sided confidence interval can also be constructed simply by replacing each zα/2 by zα in the expression for the lower or upper limit, whichever","title":"7. Product and Process Comparisons","url":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/toolaids/pff/prc.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"idle-ti.me","ref_id":"turn0search2","snippet":"# Coordinated Omission: Why Your Latency Numbers Lie ... HdrHistogram — A High Dynamic Range Histogram. hdrhistogram.org. — The data-structure work that makes honest tail-latency","title":"idle-ti.me • Coordinated Omission: Why Your Latency Numbers Lie","url":"https://idle-ti.me/blog/coordinated-omission/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"loadtest.app","ref_id":"turn0search3","snippet":"# Coordinated omission: what it is and how to avoid it ... HdrHistogram | Two methods that back-fill missing samples | Apply one of them,","title":"Coordinated omission: what it is and how to avoid it","url":"https://loadtest.app/coordinated-omission/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"hdrhistogram.github.io","ref_id":"turn0search4","snippet":"# Package org.HdrHistogram ... Recorders provide a recording API similar to Histogram, and internally maintain and coordinate active/inactive histograms such that recording remains wait-free in","title":"org.HdrHistogram (HdrHistogram 2.1.12-SNAPSHOT API)","url":"https://hdrhistogram.github.io/HdrHistogram/JavaDoc/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.publications.usace.army.mil","ref_id":"turn0search13","snippet":"Statistical Confidence Interval for Proportions. Data from a binomial distribution are composed of only two responses—“pass” or “fail.” ... The most frequent statistical interval calculated","url":"https://www.publications.usace.army.mil/Portals/76/Publications/EngineerManuals/EM_200-1-16.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"github.com","ref_id":"turn0search5","snippet":"While this model correctly measures the actual completion time of individual requests, it exhibits a strong Coordinated Omission effect, through which most of the high","title":"DeathStarBench/wrk2/README.md at master · delimitrou/DeathStarBench · GitHub","url":"https://github.com/delimitrou/DeathStarBench/blob/master/wrk2/README.md"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.nist.gov","ref_id":"turn0search6","snippet":"Probability of detection, false alarm rate, confidence intervals, confidence bounds, binomial proportion, tables for Bayesian credible intervals. ... (2020), Estimating Instrument Performance: with Co","title":"Estimating Instrument Performance: with Confidence Intervals and Confidence Bounds | NIST","url":"https://www.nist.gov/publications/estimating-instrument-performance-confidence-intervals-and-confidence-bounds"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.itl.nist.gov","ref_id":"turn0search7","snippet":"One-sided confidence intervals | A one-sided confidence interval can also be constructed simply by replacing each \\(z_{\\alpha/2}\\) by \\(z_{\\alpha}\\) in the expression for the lower","title":"7.2.4.1. Confidence intervals","url":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section2/prc241.htm"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"support.sas.com","ref_id":"turn0search8","snippet":"The lower confidence limit is set to 0 when Image: $n_1 = 0$, and the upper confidence limit is set to 1 when Image: $n_1","title":"Binomial Proportion :: Base SAS(R) 9.4 Procedures Guide: Statistical Procedures, Fourth Edition","url":"https://support.sas.com/documentation/cdl/en/procstat/68142/HTML/default/procstat_freq_details37.htm"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.osti.gov","ref_id":"turn0search14","snippet":"The number of samples needed to demonstrate the required reliability failure probability, without failures, at the 50% upper one-sided binomial confidence limit, is derived as","title":"SAND2007-5171","url":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/920126"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"deepwiki.com","ref_id":"turn0search9","snippet":"This document explains the problem of coordinated omission in performance measurements, particularly latency tracking, and how the go-hdrhistogram library addresses this issue.","title":"Coordinated Omission | elastic/go-hdrhistogram | DeepWiki","url":"https://deepwiki.com/elastic/go-hdrhistogram/4.2-coordinated-omission"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.hilarispublisher.com","ref_id":"turn0search10","snippet":"In this article, we use observed interval length and p-confidence to evaluate eight methods for finding the upper limit of a confidence interval for a","title":"On Finding the Upper Confidence Limit for a Binomial Proportion when Zero Successes are Observed | Abstract","url":"https://www.hilarispublisher.com/abstract/on-finding-the-upper-confidence-limit-for-a-binomial-proportion-when-zero-successes-are-observed-28498.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"support.ptc.com","ref_id":"turn0search11","snippet":"Zero Failure Calculations ... An upper level estimate for both one-sided and two-sided confidence limits can be obtained with r = 0. ... The probability","title":"Zero Failure Calculations","url":"https://support.ptc.com/help/wrr/r13.0.2.0/en/wrr/ReferenceGuide/appendix/zero_failure_calculations.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"ntrs.nasa.gov","ref_id":"turn0search15","snippet":"Instead, the standard approach to binomial failure analysis is to compute, for a range of possible failure rates \\(p\\), the probability of getting \\(k\\) or","title":"What does this mean in terms of a confidence interval on \\(p\\)? There appears to be a range of actual failure rates consistent with the observed failure fraction \\(k/N\\). But \\(p\\) is not a random variable (although \\(k\\) is). If it were, its PDF could be integrated to give its cumulative distribution function (CDF), then the CDF could be inverted at 0.10 and 0.90 to get the 10%–90% confidence interval. Instead, the standard approach to binomial failure analysis is to compute, for a range of possible failure rates \\(p\\), the probability of getting \\(k\\) or fewer failures, and define the confidence in terms of the resulting function of \\(p\\). For example, for \\(k = 5\\), then for each of the three subplots in figure 29, imagine adding up the heights of the columns for 0, 1, . . . , 5 failures. The result is the cumulative binomial probability and is a decreasing function of \\(p\\).","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20100038453/downloads/20100038453.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.itl.nist.gov","ref_id":"turn0search16","snippet":"Left-side italic callout: “Example showing how to calculate confidence limits”. ... UPPER = 1600/G⁻¹(0.05,4) ... Left-side italic callout: “Zero fails confidence limit calculation”. ... http://www.itl","title":"1. Exploratory Data Analysis","url":"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/toolaids/pff/E-Handbook.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.math.tecnico.ulisboa.pt","ref_id":"turn0search17","snippet":"Method I (a) | Lower limit 0 if X = 0, (α/2)1/n if X = n B if 0 < X < n X,n−X+1;α/2 |","title":"1\nConﬁdence intervals for a binomial proportion:\nc","url":"https://www.math.tecnico.ulisboa.pt/~apires/PDFs/AP_COMPSTAT02.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"en.wikipedia.org","ref_id":"turn0search18","snippet":"In statistics, a binomial proportion confidence interval is a confidence interval for the probability of success calculated from the outcome of a series of success–failure","title":"Binomial proportion confidence interval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_proportion_confidence_interval"}]}}
