{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'materialization|defn- publish|defn- open-publication|defn repl-state|index-publication' src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; sed -n '310,367p' src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc; sed -n '140,174p' src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc; sed -n '94,115p' src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc; sed -n '185,225p' collab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:236:(defn- open-publication\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:334:(defn- publish\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:2162:(defn repl-state\n                          :outcome (:dao.stream/outcome answer)})\n         false]))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n      [(assoc ix\n              :failure {:round round\n                        :stage :project\n                        :message (ex-message e)})\n       false])))\n\n\n(defn- materialize\n  \"Drain the intake into the store until it blocks.  Answers how many\n   payloads it materialized and, when the drain met a gap or a defect, what\n   it met.  The pool is not kept: its intake is dropped with the round.\"\n  [content-store pool]\n  (loop [pool pool\n         n 0]\n    (let [{:keys [signal], :as r} (jing/observe-step! content-store pool)]\n      (case signal\n        :dao.stream/ok (recur (:state r) (inc n))\n        :dao.stream/blocked [n nil]\n        [n signal]))))\n\n\n(defn- publish\n  \"Publish the covered indexes over every committed transaction through\n   the round's transactor, drain its intake into the store, and read the\n   manifest back from it.  Only a publication whose manifest the store\n   answers becomes the reported one, covering `:published` transactions;\n   in durable mode the HEAD pointer moves — `after-publish` — only after\n   that read-back, so a HEAD never names a manifest the store cannot\n   answer, and a HEAD that cannot be written leaves the round reported\n   unpublished.\"\n  [ix round]\n  (try\n    (let [{:keys [transactor pool]} (:publication ix)\n          {:keys [manifest-address]} (transactor/publish! transactor\n                                                          (:publish-opts ix))\n          [n defect] (materialize (:content-store ix) pool)]\n      (if defect\n        (assoc ix :failure {:round round :stage :materialize :outcome defect})\n        (do (index/read-manifest (:content-store ix) manifest-address)\n            (when-some [write-head! (:after-publish ix)]\n              (write-head! manifest-address))\n            (assoc ix\n                   :manifest-address manifest-address\n                   :published (:transactions ix)\n                   :published-payloads n))))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n      (assoc ix\n             :failure {:round round :stage :publish :message (ex-message e)}))))\n\n\n(defn- consume\n  \"Advance the observer past every packet still on the medium, handing each\n   to `f` with the indexer; a gap marks the indexer lost, after which no\n   packet reaches `f`.\"\n  [indexer f]\n\n\n(defn make-indexer\n  \"The indexer for one session: `observer`, attached to `program-out`\n   beside the evaluator's; `:local`, the complete-retention log its\n   transactions are committed to (dao.space.transactor requires memory-log\n   retention); and `content-store`, the `dao.jing` byte store publications\n   materialize into.  `session-token` is the shell's stable token, recorded\n   as provenance; `publish-opts` reach `transactor/publish!` as-is.\n\n   `after-publish` is `(fn [manifest-address])`, called after a round's\n   manifest is read back from the store and before the round is reported\n   published: the durable store's HEAD write, which is what makes the\n   round durably published (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md).\n   Nil — the memory store — publishes exactly as before.\n\n   Between rounds that is all it holds.  A round that has code to commit\n   opens its `:publication` — a transactor over `:local` whose intake pool\n   is one fresh complete-retention intake — and drops it when the round\n   ends, so no earlier publication's payloads stay reachable from the\n   indexer (see `step`).\"\n  [{:keys [observer session-token content-store publish-opts after-publish]}]\n  {:observer observer\n   :local (memory-log)\n   :content-store content-store\n   :session-token session-token\n   :publish-opts publish-opts\n   :after-publish after-publish\n   :next-e datom/first-user-id\n   :transactions 0\n   :published 0\n   :published-payloads 0\n   :manifest-address nil\n   :lost? false\n   :failure nil})\n  (cursor\n    [_ anchor]\n    (let [s @state]\n      ;; Every handle is the logical stream's owner: there is no\n      ;; attachment to be closed, and an owner keeps minting cursors\n      ;; after close so retained history stays readable.\n      (case anchor\n        ;; Complete retention: :oldest is the origin, before the first\n        ;; append and after close alike.\n        :dao.stream/oldest\n        (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n               :dao.stream/cursor\n               {:dao.stream.memory-log/identity (:identity s)\n                :dao.stream.memory-log/position 0})\n        :dao.stream/newest\n        (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n               :dao.stream/cursor\n               {:dao.stream.memory-log/identity (:identity s)\n                :dao.stream.memory-log/position (count (:values s))})\n        (result :dao.stream/invalid-anchor))))\n\n\n   bypass an indexer. The same applies to converting `repl/make-runner:689`\n   from run-to-completion to bounded VM execution: it is a composition\n   scheduling change, not a pump fix and not implied authorization to change\n   the workload from a fresh finite evaluation per round.\n\nIf instead `:server-pump` dominates near A's last bytes, target\n`rfc6455/take-payload:333`, `finish-frame:312`, and `concat-chunks:250`.\nPreserve the pure parser successor and owned payload semantics; improve byte\nslice/unmask/copy throughput or make assembly an explicit bounded resumable\nphase. In a resumable version retain offset/state, withhold the message event\nuntil fully assembled, report runnable work through the pump's existing\ndeadline mechanism, and deliver exactly once. Do not move codec execution\ninto the pump or deliver partial binary messages. This branch is conditional\non the trace, not a recommendation to optimize both paths speculatively.\n\nPreservation obligations for the ruling:\n\n| Existing obligation | Why this proposal can preserve it / required check |\n| --- | --- |\n| A1 owner and bounded visits | All successors are owned and advanced by the same owner; no worker callbacks, new threads, polling inside interpretation, or extra descriptor visits. Synchronous interpretation must be budgeted separately from visit count. |\n| A2 interest/deadline seam | Keep the existing `{:interest :deadline :pump!}` interface and monotonic clock; newly runnable held parser work makes the existing deadline due. Do not omit polling when a deadline is already due. |\n| A3 ordered output | No queue bypass, priority write, send-result change or byte-accounting change. Completion events/echo output retain per-connection order. |\n| A4 resource/control bounds | Preserve message-size checks before payload allocation, pong/close budgets, upgrade caps and deadlines. A resumable assembler retains at most the existing bounded message plus its explicit successor, not an unbounded side queue. |\n| A5 close/terminal behavior | Preserve first-close ownership, FIFO close placement, expiry, and exactly one terminal. Cancelling pending assembly cannot produce a later message callback after close. |\n| A6 lifecycle/isolation | Keep listener ownership, unbind grace, forced host cleanup and per-instance state; release pending derived work with its owning composition/connection. |\n| A7 | Its authoritative wording is not present in the supplied plan artifacts or source docstrings. No new wire protocol, portable transport outcome, callback contract or canonical tuple field is proposed; require the Architect to check the actual A7 text before approving. This report does not invent a seventh ruling to claim complete conformance. |\n\nThere is no demonstrated logical inconsistency in W itself. With sufficiently\nshort rounds and a transfer interval admitting 600 exchanges, its numbers are\ncompatible. This particular composition has no source-level time guarantee\nfor those rounds; a larger timeout cannot establish 10 Hz or 250 ms. The\nfresh-evaluation requirement and the synchronous all-history publication\npolicy may be incompatible with W on the measured host, but that is a\nmeasurable composition result, not a proof that the registered workload is\nuniversally unsatisfiable. “Within ~60 seconds” also lacks an exact upper\ntolerance in the available brief; a five-minute safety cap must not become\nthat tolerance.\n\n## Remote verification and decisive observations\n\nFirst run attribution on the orchestrator's qualified remote checkout:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
